🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>JDK vs. JRE? The JDK has the compiler and development tools; a runtime alone can only run compiled code.</li> <li>How do you run a single Java file without compiling first? <code>java File.java</code>, the source-file launcher from Java 11.</li> <li>Where do tests go in a Maven project? <code>src/test/java</code>.</li> <li>What does <code>mvn package</code> do? Runs the lifecycle up to package: compile, test, then build the jar.</li> <li>What's an LTS release? A release with long-term support updates, such as 17, 21 and 25.</li> </ol> <ol> <li><code>--release</code> vs. <code>-source</code>/<code>-target</code>? <code>--release</code> also checks you only use APIs available in the target version.</li> <li>What does "class file has wrong version 65.0" mean? Code compiled for Java 21 is running on an older JVM.</li> <li>How do you make builds reproducible? Pin the JDK, plugins and dependencies; use the Maven Wrapper.</li> <li>How do you pass a system property vs. a program argument? <code>-Dkey=value</code> before the class or file; arguments after it.</li> <li>When would you use JShell? To try one API or expression quickly.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How would you plan a Java 17 → 21 upgrade for 40 services? Pin toolchains, upgrade build images first, fix deprecations, roll out service by service with canaries.</li> <li>CI is green but production fails with <code>NoSuchMethodError</code>. Where do you look? Build vs. runtime JDK mismatch and dependency version conflicts on the classpath.</li> <li>How do you keep developer setup consistent across a large team? Dev containers or a version file (<code>.sdkmanrc</code>), the Maven Wrapper and a single onboarding script.</li> <li>Maven or Gradle for a new organisation-wide standard? Either works; choose on team skills, build speed and plugin needs, then standardise on one.</li> <li>Now the code must also run on Android. What changes? Android's runtime supports a subset of newer Java features and APIs; check desugaring support before using them.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a fourth test to <code>DeliverySlotTest</code> proving a negative cutoff is rejected.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>@Test
void rejectsNegativeCutoff() {
    assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
            () -&gt; noon.isOpenAt(LocalTime.of(9, 0), -5));
}
</code></pre> <code>mvn test</code> then reports <code>Tests run: 4, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0</code>.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is a design pattern? A reusable, named solution to a recurring design problem, described as roles and collaborations rather than code.</li> <li>Name the three GoF categories. Creational, structural and behavioural.</li> <li>Who are the Gang of Four? Gamma, Helm, Johnson and Vlissides, authors of Design Patterns (1994), which catalogued 23 patterns.</li> <li>Pattern vs. algorithm? An algorithm is a precise sequence of steps; a pattern is a structure of classes and responsibilities you adapt.</li> <li>Give one pattern per category. Builder (creational), Adapter (structural), Strategy (behavioural).</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you decide whether to apply a pattern? Identify a concrete, current problem and check the pattern's consequences are worth it.</li> <li>Which GoF patterns have become simpler in modern Java, and why? Strategy, Command and Observer via lambdas; Visitor via sealed types and pattern matching.</li> <li>Pattern vs. architectural style? Patterns structure a few classes; styles structure whole applications (layered, hexagonal).</li> <li>What's an idiom? Give a Java example. A language-specific pattern, such as try-with-resources or the lazy holder singleton.</li> <li>How would you spot over-engineering in a review? Interfaces with one implementation and no test double, factories that build one type, layers that only delegate.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Your team overuses patterns. How do you change that? Make simplicity a review criterion, require a stated problem for each abstraction, and delete unused indirection in refactoring time.</li> <li>How do patterns help a 200-engineer organisation? Shared vocabulary and reference implementations make designs reviewable and code predictable across teams.</li> <li>Are design patterns still relevant with frameworks like Spring? Yes: frameworks are built from patterns (proxies, templates, factories), and you need them to use and debug the framework.</li> <li>Map a legacy subsystem to patterns: why bother? It gives names for refactoring steps and reveals half-implemented patterns that cause bugs.</li> <li>Now the same Strategy must be chosen per tenant at runtime across 50 services. What changes? It becomes a configuration and feature-flag problem: the selection moves out of code into versioned config, with safe defaults and observability on which strategy ran.</li> </ol> <p>Task: In Example 1, add a <code>WhatsAppNotifier</code> and make unknown channels fall back to email instead of throwing.</p> Solution <pre><code>static final class WhatsAppNotifier implements Notifier {
    public String send(String customer, String message) { return "WhatsApp to " + customer + ": " + message; }
}

// in main
Map&lt;String, Notifier&gt; notifiers = Map.of(
        "sms", new SmsNotifier(), "email", new EmailNotifier(),
        "push", new PushNotifier(), "whatsapp", new WhatsAppNotifier());
Notifier fallback = notifiers.get("email");
System.out.println(notifiers.getOrDefault("fax", fallback).send("Asha", "Order placed"));
// Email to Asha: Order placed
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Why learn design patterns? For a shared vocabulary and proven solutions that make code easier to change.</li> <li>What is YAGNI? "You aren't gonna need it": don't build features or abstractions before they're needed.</li> <li>What is over-engineering? Adding complexity for requirements that don't exist.</li> <li>How does Strategy help when rules change often? New rules are new classes, so existing code isn't edited.</li> <li>Give a pattern that lambdas made smaller. Strategy (or Command): a functional interface plus a lambda replaces a class per variant.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you find the axes of change in a code base? Look at change history (<code>git log</code>, hot-spot analysis) and incident reports.</li> <li>When would you use a sealed interface with <code>switch</code> instead of polymorphism? When the set of types is closed and owned by you and you add operations more often than types.</li> <li>What's speculative generality? How do you fix it? Abstraction for imagined needs; inline it and reintroduce on real demand.</li> <li>Rule of three? Tolerate duplication twice; refactor on the third occurrence.</li> <li>What makes it safe to delay adding a pattern? Good tests and small modules, so refactoring later is cheap.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>A team wants a generic framework "for future flexibility". How do you respond? Ask for evidence of the variation, estimate the carrying cost, and propose the smallest reversible step.</li> <li>How do you decide extension points for a public library? Public APIs are hard to change, so add extension points for documented, demanded use cases and keep the rest internal.</li> <li>How do you measure if a pattern pays off? Lead time for changes on that area, defect rate, and review time before and after.</li> <li>Expression problem: explain and relate to Visitor. Adding types vs. operations; Visitor makes operations cheap and types expensive, like sealed types with <code>switch</code>.</li> <li>Now 10 teams extend the same promotion engine. What changes? You need stable contracts, versioning, contract tests and ownership of the extension API.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>Cancelled(String orderId, boolean refunded)</code> event to Example 3 and handle it in both operations.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>sealed interface DeliveryEvent permits Assigned, PickedUp, Delivered, Failed, Cancelled { String orderId(); }
record Cancelled(String orderId, boolean refunded) implements DeliveryEvent { }
enum Kind { ASSIGNED, PICKED_UP, DELIVERED, FAILED, CANCELLED }

// customerMessage(...)
case Cancelled c when c.refunded() -&gt; c.orderId() + " was cancelled and refunded";
case Cancelled c -&gt; c.orderId() + " was cancelled";

// kind(...)
case Cancelled c -&gt; Kind.CANCELLED;
</code></pre> Until both switches handle <code>Cancelled</code>, the compiler reports that the <code>switch</code> expression doesn't cover all possible input values.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does Adapter do? Converts one interface into another that clients expect.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. <code>InputStreamReader</code>, <code>Arrays.asList</code>, <code>Enumeration.asIterator()</code>.</li> <li>Object vs class adapter? Composition vs inheritance; object adapters are more flexible.</li> <li>Why wrap a vendor SDK instead of using it directly? To keep vendor details out of the domain and swap vendors easily.</li> <li>Adapter vs Decorator? Adapter changes the interface; Decorator keeps it and adds behaviour.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>What should an adapter translate besides method names? Units, types, nulls, exceptions, codes and identifiers.</li> <li>How do you test adapters? Contract tests shared by real adapters and fakes, plus sandbox integration tests.</li> <li>What's a two-way adapter for? Gradual migration between old and new interfaces.</li> <li>How do you handle unknown vendor codes? Map to an explicit UNKNOWN, alert and count.</li> <li>Where do retries belong? In a decorator or a template around the adapter, not inside it.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design an integration layer for three payment providers. A <code>PaymentGateway</code> port, one adapter per provider, shared contract tests, routing strategy, idempotency and reconciliation.</li> <li>What is an anti-corruption layer and when is it worth it? Translation boundary protecting your model from another system's; worth it when the other model is messy or changes independently.</li> <li>How do you handle vendor API version upgrades safely? Adapter per version, contract tests, shadow traffic, gradual switch.</li> <li>How does hexagonal architecture relate to Adapter? Ports are targets; infrastructure classes are adapters.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic and the vendor rate-limits you. What changes? Rate-limiter and queue decorators, batching if supported, multi-vendor routing.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a second payment adapter for a vendor whose SDK returns a <code>Map&lt;String, String&gt;</code> like <code>{status=OK, id=...}</code> or <code>{status=DECLINED, why=NO_FUNDS}</code>, and make it pass <code>PaymentGatewayContractTest</code>.</p> Solution <pre><code>final class MapPayGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    private final MapPaySdk sdk;
    MapPayGateway(MapPaySdk sdk) { this.sdk = sdk; }
    public ChargeResult charge(ChargeRequest r) {
        if (r.amountCents() &lt;= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("amount must be positive");
        Map&lt;String, String&gt; res = sdk.pay(r.idempotencyKey(), r.amountCents(), r.customerId());
        return switch (res.getOrDefault("status", "")) {
            case "OK" -&gt; new Approved(res.get("id"));
            case "DECLINED" -&gt; new Declined(res.get("why").equals("NO_FUNDS") ? DeclineReason.INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS : DeclineReason.OTHER);
            default -&gt; new Failed("MapPay " + res, false);
        };
    }
}
// class MapPayGatewayContractTest extends PaymentGatewayContractTest { gateway() { return new MapPayGateway(new MapPaySdk()); } }
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What problem does Builder solve? Many parameters, many optional: telescoping constructors and half-built JavaBeans.</li> <li>Where do you validate? In <code>build()</code> (and the record's compact constructor).</li> <li>Why return <code>this</code> from builder methods? To allow method chaining (fluent API).</li> <li>Give a JDK builder. <code>HttpRequest.newBuilder()</code>, <code>StringBuilder</code>, <code>Locale.Builder</code>, <code>Stream.builder()</code>.</li> <li>Builder vs constructor? Constructor for few, required parameters; builder for many or optional ones.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>GoF Builder vs Bloch's builder? GoF: director + builder interface, many representations; Bloch: fluent builder for one immutable class.</li> <li>How do you enforce required fields at compile time? A step builder with one interface per required field.</li> <li>What can go wrong with collections in builders? The product shares the builder's mutable list; fix with <code>List.copyOf</code>.</li> <li>Lombok <code>@Builder</code> downsides? No required-field enforcement, invisible code, <code>@Builder.Default</code> surprises.</li> <li>Builder vs Abstract Factory? Builder builds one complex object step by step; Abstract Factory creates families of objects, each in one call.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Designing a public SDK's request builders: what rules do you set? Required in <code>builder()</code>, optional as methods, validation in <code>build()</code>, immutable products, additive evolution only.</li> <li>Where should business validation live: builder or service? Shape and invariants in the product/builder; I/O-dependent rules in services.</li> <li>How do builders help test suites at scale? Test-data builders isolate tests from constructor changes and make intent obvious.</li> <li>Builders and serialization (Jackson): how do you keep immutability? <code>@JsonDeserialize(builder = ...)</code> or record constructors; never add setters.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic and the builder shows up in allocation profiles. What do you do? Confirm with JFR/async-profiler, check escape analysis, then use constructors or factories in the hot path only.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>Address.toString()</code> in <code>AddressDemo</code> mask the gate code, without losing it.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>record Address(String line1, String pincode, String line2, String landmark, String gateCode) {
    @Override public String toString() {
        String masked = gateCode.isEmpty() ? "" : "****";
        return "Address[line1=%s, pincode=%s, line2=%s, landmark=%s, gateCode=%s]"
                .formatted(line1, pincode, line2, landmark, masked);
    }
}
</code></pre> The accessor <code>gateCode()</code> still returns the real value for the rider's app.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Decorator? A wrapper with the same interface that adds behaviour and delegates.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. <code>BufferedInputStream</code>, <code>GZIPInputStream</code>, <code>Collections.unmodifiableList</code>.</li> <li>Why not use inheritance? Combination explosion and compile-time-only choices.</li> <li>Can decorators wrap decorators? Yes, that's the point: they stack.</li> <li>Decorator vs Adapter? Decorator keeps the interface; Adapter changes it.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why does decorator order matter? Outer layers see and control what inner layers do (retry/log, breaker/retry).</li> <li>Decorator vs Proxy? Same structure; Proxy controls access, Decorator adds behaviour.</li> <li>Why don't Spring proxies intercept self-calls? The call goes to <code>this</code>, not the proxy.</li> <li>What's the risk with default methods? Decorators inherit defaults instead of forwarding, bypassing the inner object.</li> <li>How do you test a decorator? With a fake inner component and controlled time.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design a resilience stack for a payment call. Timeout per attempt, limited retries only for idempotent calls, circuit breaker, bulkhead, metrics outermost.</li> <li>When do hand-written decorators stop scaling? Many interfaces or methods; use AOP, interceptors or code generation.</li> <li>How do you make a caching decorator safe? TTLs, tenant-aware keys, cache only successes, eviction on change, stampede protection.</li> <li>How do you roll out a new decorator safely? Feature-flag it in wiring, shadow mode for caches, dashboards per layer.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic and the breaker opens constantly. What do you check? Thresholds vs normal error rate, timeouts, downstream capacity, and whether retries amplify load.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Write a <code>Timing</code> decorator for <code>InventoryClient</code> that records each call's duration using an injected clock.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>record Timing(InventoryClient inner, java.util.function.Supplier&lt;java.time.Instant&gt; clock,
              java.util.List&lt;java.time.Duration&gt; durations) implements InventoryClient {
    public int available(String store, String sku) {
        var start = clock.get();
        try { return inner.available(store, sku); }
        finally { durations.add(java.time.Duration.between(start, clock.get())); }
    }
}
</code></pre> <code>finally</code> records failures too; the injected clock keeps tests deterministic.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Facade? A simple, unified interface over a set of subsystem classes.</li> <li>Does a facade prevent using subsystems directly? No, they stay usable.</li> <li>Give a Java example. SLF4J <code>LoggerFactory</code>, <code>HttpClient</code>, Spring <code>JdbcClient</code>.</li> <li>Facade vs Adapter? Facade simplifies many; Adapter converts one interface.</li> <li>What is a god facade? One facade fronting everything, with too many dependencies and owners.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Facade vs service layer? Facade simplifies access; service layer owns business rules and transactions.</li> <li>How do you keep facades small? Split by use-case area and team ownership.</li> <li>How do facades help modular monoliths? They're the public API of a module, enforced with JPMS or ArchUnit.</li> <li>What should a facade return? DTOs or result types, not internal entities or exceptions.</li> <li>How do you handle partial failure in a multi-step facade? Compensation in reverse order, plus idempotency.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design "place order" across services. Saga with compensations, idempotency keys, outbox events, and clear critical vs non-critical steps.</li> <li>BFF vs general-purpose API: when do you choose BFF? When client needs differ significantly and teams own their screens.</li> <li>How do you migrate a module behind its facade into a microservice? Keep the interface, replace the implementation with a remote client, add resilience.</li> <li>How do you stop a facade becoming a latency trap? Parallel calls, timeouts, fallbacks, per-step metrics.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic and the home screen facade overloads downstream services. What do you do? Caching per section, request coalescing, lazy loading, degrade non-critical sections.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>DeliveryQuoteFacade</code> call slots and ETA in parallel.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>Quote quote(String pincode, long basketCents) {
    Optional&lt;String&gt; store = serviceability.storeFor(pincode);
    if (store.isEmpty()) return new NotServiceable(pincode, "We don't deliver to " + pincode + " yet");
    String s = store.get();
    var slotsF = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; slots.openSlots(s));
    var etaF = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; eta.expressMinutes(s));
    List&lt;SlotOption&gt; options = slotsF.join().stream()
            .map(t -&gt; new SlotOption(t + "-" + t.plusHours(1), fees.fee(s, basketCents, t))).toList();
    return new Available(s, etaF.join(), options);
}
</code></pre> In production, add timeouts (<code>orTimeout</code>) and fallbacks for each future.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What problem does Factory Method solve? It decouples callers from concrete classes, so the created type can vary.</li> <li>What are the roles? Product, concrete product, creator, concrete creator.</li> <li>Give a JDK example of a static factory. <code>List.of</code>, <code>Integer.valueOf</code>, <code>LocalDate.of</code>.</li> <li>Static factory vs constructor? Static factories have names, can cache, can return subtypes; constructors can't.</li> <li>Is a simple factory a GoF pattern? No; it's a common idiom. Factory Method uses subclasses.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Factory Method vs Abstract Factory? Factory Method creates one product via a method; Abstract Factory creates families of related products via an object.</li> <li>How does Factory Method relate to Template Method? The factory method is often one step of a template method.</li> <li>How do you keep a parameterised factory extensible? Use a registry (<code>Map&lt;Key, Supplier&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>) or <code>ServiceLoader</code>.</li> <li>Why avoid <code>default</code> in an enum <code>switch</code> factory? It hides missing cases from the compiler.</li> <li>When would you inject a <code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code>? When code needs new instances on demand and tests need control (IDs, buffers).</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design a payment gateway factory for 5,000 merchants across regions. Config-driven registry, per-merchant cached clients, environment guards, startup warm-up of top merchants, metrics on creation failures.</li> <li>Factories vs DI containers: what still needs a factory? Runtime choices by key or request, which the container can't know at wiring time.</li> <li>How would you let other teams add exporters without touching your code? <code>ServiceLoader</code> or a registration API with a stable product interface and contract tests.</li> <li><code>computeIfAbsent</code> with slow creation under load: what happens? Threads for the same bin block; move slow I/O out or use futures as values.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic, and clients are created per request. What do you change? Cache per key, add pooling, and pre-create hot clients.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>whatsapp</code> channel to <code>NotifierFactoryDemo</code> and make the factory reject blank channel names.</p> Solution <pre><code>record WhatsApp() implements Notifier {
    public String send(String to, String t) { return "WHATSAPP " + to + ": " + t; }
}

static Notifier notifierFor(String channel) {
    if (channel == null || channel.isBlank()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("channel is required");
    return switch (channel.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)) {
        case "sms" -&gt; new Sms();
        case "push" -&gt; new Push();
        case "whatsapp" -&gt; new WhatsApp();
        default -&gt; throw new IllegalArgumentException("unknown channel " + channel);
    };
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Observer? A one-to-many dependency: the subject notifies observers of changes.</li> <li>Give a Java example. Swing <code>ActionListener</code>, Spring <code>@EventListener</code>, <code>PropertyChangeListener</code>.</li> <li>Why is <code>java.util.Observable</code> not used? Deprecated since Java 9: a class not an interface, not thread-safe, limited event model.</li> <li>What is a lapsed listener? An observer never unsubscribed, kept alive by the subject: a memory leak.</li> <li>Push vs pull? Push sends data in the event; pull sends a notification and observers query the subject.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>What happens if an observer throws? In a naive loop, the rest are skipped and the publisher fails; isolate with per-observer <code>try/catch</code>.</li> <li>Sync vs async observers: trade-offs? Latency and isolation vs ordering, transactions and lost errors.</li> <li>How does Spring avoid emails for rolled-back orders? <code>@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)</code>.</li> <li>Observer vs Pub/Sub? Observer knows the subject; Pub/Sub uses a broker and topics.</li> <li>What does Flow add? Back-pressure via <code>Subscription.request(n)</code> and async delivery.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Make order events reliable across services. Transactional outbox, broker with per-order partitioning, idempotent consumers, dead-letter queues and replay.</li> <li>How do you keep event-driven flows understandable? Event catalogue, tracing with correlation IDs, and orchestration for complex processes.</li> <li>How do you evolve event schemas? Additive changes, versioned types, schema registry, consumers tolerant of unknown fields.</li> <li>A consumer falls behind by hours. What do you do? Check lag metrics, scale consumers to partition count, apply back-pressure or shed non-critical work.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic and the in-process bus queue grows unbounded. Fix it. Bound the queue, decide block vs drop vs reject, move heavy subscribers to a broker.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make the loyalty subscriber idempotent so a duplicate <code>Delivered</code> event doesn't award points twice.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>static final java.util.Set&lt;String&gt; processed = java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();

static void loyalty(OrderEvent e) {
    if (e instanceof Delivered d &amp;&amp; processed.add(d.orderId())) {   // add() is false for duplicates
        System.out.println("loyalty: points for " + d.customerId());
    }
}
</code></pre> In production the "processed" set lives in the loyalty database, updated in the same transaction as the points.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is encapsulation? Keeping an object's state private and changing it only through methods, so the object can enforce its invariants.</li> <li>Interface vs. abstract class? An interface is a contract a class can implement many of; an abstract class can hold state and constructors but a class can extend only one.</li> <li>What is polymorphism? One call with different behaviour depending on the runtime type of the object, decided by dynamic dispatch.</li> <li>Overloading vs. overriding? Overloading picks a method at compile time by parameter types; overriding replaces a method at runtime in a subclass.</li> <li>Why prefer composition over inheritance? It avoids tight coupling to a superclass's implementation and lets behaviour change at runtime.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>A getter returns an internal list and data gets corrupted. How do you find and fix it? Search for mutations on returned collections, add a test that mutates the returned value, then return an unmodifiable view or copy.</li> <li>When would you make a class <code>final</code>? By default, unless it's designed and documented for inheritance; it protects invariants and lets you change internals freely.</li> <li>How do you design a class so tests don't need a database? Depend on an interface for persistence and pass a fake in the test via constructor injection.</li> <li>Records: when not to use them? When the type needs mutable state or hidden fields, or when its <code>toString</code> would leak secrets.</li> <li>Explain the fragile base class problem. A subclass depends on the superclass's self-use of overridable methods, so changes in the superclass break it.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Your team's domain model is anaemic; services have all the logic. What's your migration plan? Pick one aggregate, move rules into behaviour methods, migrate callers incrementally, then make setters private; measure defect rate per area.</li> <li>Polymorphism or a sealed <code>switch</code> for payment methods? Depends on who adds variants: external plugins favour polymorphism; a closed set you own favours sealed types with exhaustive switches.</li> <li>Does interface-heavy design hurt performance at 10x traffic? Usually not: monomorphic call sites inline. Profile first; only megamorphic hot paths matter.</li> <li>How do you keep a published library extensible without inheritance? Expose interfaces and composition hooks, keep classes final, use default methods to evolve.</li> <li>Now the basket is shared across devices (phone and web). What changes? Identity and concurrency control (versioning/optimistic locking) become part of the entity's invariants.</li> </ol> <p>Task: <code>Order</code> has public fields <code>status</code> and <code>refundedAmount</code>. Make it impossible to refund more than the order total or to refund a cancelled order.</p> Solution <pre><code>final class Order {
    enum Status { PLACED, DELIVERED, CANCELLED }

    private final long totalInCents;
    private Status status = Status.PLACED;
    private long refundedInCents;

    Order(long totalInCents) {
        if (totalInCents &lt;= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("total must be positive");
        this.totalInCents = totalInCents;
    }

    void cancel() { status = Status.CANCELLED; }

    void refund(long amountInCents) {
        if (status == Status.CANCELLED) throw new IllegalStateException("cancelled orders can't be refunded");
        if (amountInCents &lt;= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("refund must be positive");
        if (refundedInCents + amountInCents &gt; totalInCents) throw new IllegalStateException("refund exceeds total");
        refundedInCents += amountInCents;
    }

    long refundedInCents() { return refundedInCents; }
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does DRY mean? Every piece of knowledge has one authoritative representation; it's about rules, not text.</li> <li>What's YAGNI? Don't build things before they're needed.</li> <li>What's the Law of Demeter? Only talk to immediate collaborators: fields, parameters, objects you create.</li> <li>Tell, Don't Ask? Tell objects to act instead of asking for their data and deciding for them.</li> <li>High cohesion, low coupling? Related things together, few dependencies between modules.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>When should you NOT remove duplication? When the copies change for different reasons or belong to different owners.</li> <li>Is <code>list.stream().filter().map()</code> a Demeter violation? No: it's a fluent API on one abstraction.</li> <li>How does Tell, Don't Ask help with concurrency? It puts check and act in one method, avoiding check-then-act races.</li> <li>Which GRASP principle helps most day to day? Information Expert: give the responsibility to the class with the data.</li> <li>YAGNI exceptions? Security, data models and public API contracts, which are costly to change later.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Your organisation has a 400-class shared library. What do you do? Split by owner, version independently, move domain rules into owning services.</li> <li>How do you balance DRY with service autonomy? Share contracts, not implementations; accept duplicated code across boundaries.</li> <li>How do you spot the wrong abstraction in review? Boolean flags, type switches and special cases accumulating in shared code.</li> <li>Which decisions deserve up-front design despite YAGNI? One-way doors: data formats, public APIs, security architecture.</li> <li>Now rider payouts differ per city and per vehicle. What changes? Payout policy becomes data-driven configuration per city/vehicle, still owned by Rider Ops, with validation at load time.</li> </ol> <p>Task: In Example 2, add a daily spending limit to the wallet without changing <code>Order</code> or <code>Customer</code>.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>static final class Wallet {
    private long balanceInCents;
    private final long dailyLimitInCents;
    private long spentTodayInCents;

    Wallet(long balance, long dailyLimit) { this.balanceInCents = balance; this.dailyLimitInCents = dailyLimit; }

    boolean tryDebit(long cents) {
        if (cents &lt;= 0 || cents &gt; balanceInCents) return false;
        if (spentTodayInCents + cents &gt; dailyLimitInCents) return false;
        balanceInCents -= cents;
        spentTodayInCents += cents;
        return true;
    }
}
</code></pre> Only the wallet changed, because the decision lives there (Tell, Don't Ask).</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is the Singleton pattern? It ensures one instance of a class and gives a global access point to it.</li> <li>How do you stop others creating instances? A private constructor.</li> <li>What's the simplest thread-safe Singleton in Java? An enum with one constant, or a <code>static final</code> field.</li> <li>Eager vs lazy? Eager creates the instance at class initialisation; lazy creates it on first use.</li> <li>Why is Singleton criticised? It's global state: hidden dependencies and hard-to-test code.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why must the field be <code>volatile</code> in double-checked locking? Without it, another thread can see a partially constructed object (no happens-before).</li> <li>How does the lazy holder idiom achieve thread safety? Through the JVM's class-initialisation guarantees: the holder is initialised once, under a lock, on first access.</li> <li>How can a Singleton be broken? Reflection, serialization, cloning and multiple class loaders.</li> <li>How do you make a serializable Singleton safe? Implement <code>readResolve</code>, or use an enum.</li> <li>Singleton vs Spring singleton bean? A Spring bean is one per container, created and injected by Spring; a GoF singleton is one per class loader, reached statically.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Your codebase has 40 static singletons. How do you remove them safely? Introduce interfaces, make <code>getInstance()</code> return a container-installed instance, migrate callers to constructor injection, then delete the accessors.</li> <li>When is a static singleton still right? Process-wide, stateless or thread-safe infrastructure with no composition root: a library's internal registry, a global metrics registry.</li> <li>A singleton cache works in one region. Now make it multi-region. Uniqueness becomes per region or per tenant; data may need a distributed cache with invalidation, and the local instance becomes a near-cache.</li> <li>Static singletons and class-initialisation deadlocks: explain. Two threads initialise two classes whose static initialisers reference each other in opposite orders and wait forever on the class-init locks.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic, and the singleton's <code>synchronized</code> method is a hotspot. What do you do? Profile, then replace the lock with concurrent structures (<code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>, <code>LongAdder</code>), shard state, or make the object immutable and swap it atomically.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>MetricsRegistryDemo.Metrics</code> testable without changing its callers' behaviour in production.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>interface MetricsSink { void increment(String name); }

enum Metrics implements MetricsSink { INSTANCE; /* existing code */ }

static final class Checkout {
    private final MetricsSink metrics;
    Checkout() { this(Metrics.INSTANCE); }           // production default
    Checkout(MetricsSink metrics) { this.metrics = metrics; }  // tests pass a fake
    void place(String orderId) { metrics.increment("checkout.orders_placed"); }
}
</code></pre> Production code keeps using the singleton through the default constructor; tests inject a recording fake.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does SOLID stand for? Single responsibility, open/closed, Liskov substitution, interface segregation, dependency inversion.</li> <li>Explain SRP. A class should have one reason to change, serving one actor.</li> <li>Give an LSP violation. Square extending a mutable Rectangle: setting the width changes the height.</li> <li>What is ISP? Clients shouldn't depend on methods they don't use; prefer small role interfaces.</li> <li>How does DIP help testing? Depending on interfaces lets tests pass fakes instead of real infrastructure.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>DIP vs. dependency injection? DIP is who owns the abstraction (the policy side); DI is the technique of passing dependencies in.</li> <li>How do you decide where to apply OCP? Use change history and the roadmap; apply on the axis that keeps changing.</li> <li>Is <code>Collections.unmodifiableList</code> an LSP violation? Arguably yes; the JDK documents mutators as optional operations, a deliberate trade-off.</li> <li>How would you test time-based rules? Inject a <code>Clock</code> and use <code>Clock.fixed</code> in tests, including at the exact boundary.</li> <li>Signs a class breaks SRP? Many unrelated imports, frequent merge conflicts, changes requested by different teams.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do SOLID principles show up at service level? SRP → service boundaries by business capability; DIP → ports and adapters; ISP → narrow APIs per consumer.</li> <li>When is applying SOLID wrong? For throwaway or very stable code, or when abstractions are added for imagined change.</li> <li>Design idempotent refunds across retries and provider timeouts. Idempotency key end to end, ledger before/after with a unique constraint, and pass the key to the provider.</li> <li>How do you raise SOLID awareness in 10 teams without dogma? Review real incidents and change costs, add review questions rather than rules.</li> <li>Now refunds come from three channels (app, support, chat bot). What changes? A single refund policy behind one port, channel-specific adapters, and per-channel limits as configuration.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a "loyalty points" pricing rule to Example 2 that takes 1% off for customers with more than 1,000 points, without changing <code>OrderService</code>.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>record LoyaltyDiscount(java.util.Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; pointsByEmail) implements PricingRule {
    public long adjust(Order o, long total) {
        int points = pointsByEmail.getOrDefault(o.customerEmail(), 0);
        return points &gt; 1_000 ? total - o.basketInCents() / 100 : total;
    }
}
// main: rules.add(new LoyaltyDiscount(Map.of("ravi@example.com", 1_500)));
</code></pre> <code>OrderService</code> is untouched: the change is one new class and one line in the composition root.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Strategy? A family of interchangeable algorithms behind one interface, used by a context.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. <code>Comparator</code> passed to <code>List.sort</code>.</li> <li>How do lambdas relate to Strategy? A lambda can implement a functional interface, so it is a strategy.</li> <li>What principle does Strategy support? Open/Closed: new algorithms without changing the context.</li> <li>When is an <code>if</code> better? Two simple, stable branches.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Strategy vs State? Strategy is chosen by the client; State changes itself as the object's lifecycle moves.</li> <li>Strategy vs Template Method? Composition vs inheritance; Strategy swaps the whole algorithm at runtime.</li> <li>How do you select a strategy at runtime? Registry map, context-based factory, or DI; fail loudly on unknown keys.</li> <li>Why keep strategies stateless? So one instance is safe for all threads and results don't depend on history.</li> <li>How would Spring give you all strategies? Inject <code>Map&lt;String, Strategy&gt;</code> or <code>List&lt;Strategy&gt;</code> of beans.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Roll out a new pricing strategy to 5% of orders safely. Stable bucketing by order/customer, guardrail metrics, shadow mode first, instant rollback switch.</li> <li>How do you compare two strategies fairly? Randomised, stable assignment; compare business metrics with confidence intervals, not anecdotes.</li> <li>Strategy explosion: how do you control it? Parameterise strategies, keep numbers in config, delete losers after experiments.</li> <li>When would you introduce a rules engine instead? When non-engineers change rules frequently and need audit and versioning.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic, and one strategy calls a slow ML service. What do you do? Timeouts and a fallback strategy, cache features, and degrade gracefully to the simple strategy.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>PEAK_HOURS</code> delivery-fee strategy to <code>InterfaceStrategyDemo</code> that adds $20.00 between 18:00 and 21:00 on top of another strategy.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>record PeakHours(DeliveryFeeStrategy base, java.time.Clock clock) implements DeliveryFeeStrategy {
    public long feeCents(Basket b) {
        var now = java.time.LocalTime.now(clock);
        boolean peak = !now.isBefore(java.time.LocalTime.of(18, 0)) &amp;&amp; now.isBefore(java.time.LocalTime.of(21, 0));
        return base.feeCents(b) + (peak ? 2_000 : 0);
    }
}
</code></pre> It wraps another strategy (composition) and takes a <code>Clock</code>, so tests can fix the time.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Template Method? A fixed algorithm skeleton in a base class, with steps filled in by subclasses.</li> <li>Why make the template method <code>final</code>? So subclasses can't change the order or skip steps.</li> <li>What is a hook? An optional step with a default that subclasses may override.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. <code>AbstractList</code> (implement <code>get</code>, <code>size</code>), <code>InputStream.read()</code>.</li> <li>What's the Hollywood Principle? "Don't call us, we'll call you": the framework calls your code.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Template Method vs Strategy? Inheritance and fixed structure vs composition and swappable whole algorithms.</li> <li>How would you implement it without inheritance? Pass steps as lambdas or a small interface (callback style, like <code>JdbcTemplate</code>).</li> <li>What's the fragile base class problem? Changes in the base class break subclasses that depend on its internal behaviour.</li> <li>Why not call abstract steps from a constructor? Subclass fields aren't initialised yet.</li> <li>How does Factory Method relate? A factory method is often one step of a template method.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design a resilient partner-call framework. Final template with validation, classified retries with backoff and jitter, error mapping, metrics, per-partner hooks.</li> <li>When do internal frameworks go wrong? Too many hooks, deep hierarchies, forced inheritance; fix with composition and small APIs.</li> <li>How do you migrate from inheritance templates to callbacks? Add a callback-based template, adapt old subclasses to it, migrate callers incrementally.</li> <li>How do you keep templates evolvable? Add hooks, never remove; keep steps few; version the framework.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic and retries amplify partner outages. What do you change? Retry budgets, jitter, circuit breakers, and lower attempt counts under load.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add jitter to <code>PartnerCallTemplateDemo</code> backoff (±20%) without making tests random.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>interface Jitter { double factor(); }                      // returns 0.8..1.2 in production

// in PartnerCall: inject Jitter alongside Sleeper
Duration jittered = Duration.ofMillis(Math.round(delay.toMillis() * jitter.factor()));
sleeper.sleep(jittered);

// production: () -&gt; 0.8 + ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextDouble() * 0.4
// tests:      () -&gt; 1.0
</code></pre> Randomness is injected like time, so tests stay deterministic.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Abstract Factory? An interface for creating families of related objects without naming concrete classes.</li> <li>Give an example family. Country kit: tax, address format, currency formatter.</li> <li>Abstract Factory vs Factory Method? Family via an object vs one product via a subclass.</li> <li>Name a JDK example. <code>DocumentBuilderFactory</code>, <code>TransformerFactory</code>.</li> <li>What's easy and what's hard to add? New families easy; new product types hard.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you choose the factory? Once at startup from config (or per request for per-customer families), and inject it.</li> <li>Why avoid default methods for new products? They silently give families a generic product.</li> <li>How do Spring profiles relate? A profile's <code>@Configuration</code> acts as a concrete factory for a set of beans.</li> <li>How do you stop clients mixing families? Hide concrete classes, inject only the factory, and tag and check products.</li> <li>How do you test families? A contract test run against every factory.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design multi-country, multi-environment payments. Independent families (country kit, payment stack) composed at startup; tagging and validation; contract tests.</li> <li>How do you prevent sandbox/live mixups? One config source per environment, refuse test keys in prod, assembly checks, startup alerts.</li> <li>When do families explode combinatorially, and what do you do? Several axes; split into orthogonal families and compose.</li> <li>How would third parties add a family? <code>ServiceLoader</code> or plugin API with contract tests.</li> <li>Now 10x tenants: one factory per tenant? Cache stateless families, create resource-holding products lazily, and bound per-tenant resources.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a Saudi Arabia kit to <code>RegionalCheckoutDemo</code> with 15% VAT, a five-digit postcode rule and a "within 90 minutes" promise.</p> Solution <pre><code>static final class SaudiKit implements CountryKit {
    public String code() { return "SA"; }
    public TaxPolicy tax() { return i -&gt; i.cents() * 15 / 100; }
    public AddressFormatter addresses() { return new AddressFormatter() {
        public String format(Address a) { return a.line1() + ", " + a.city() + " " + a.postcode() + ", KSA"; }
        public boolean validPostcode(String p) { return p.matches("\\d{5}"); } }; }
    public DeliveryPromise delivery() { return m -&gt; "within 90 minutes"; }
}
// KITS = Map.of("IN", new IndiaKit(), "AE", new UaeKit(), "SA", new SaudiKit());
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does Bridge do? Separates an abstraction from its implementation so both can vary independently.</li> <li>What problem does it solve? Class explosion: m × n subclasses for two dimensions.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. JDBC: <code>Connection</code> API vs vendor <code>Driver</code>s.</li> <li>What connects the two hierarchies? A field in the abstraction referencing the implementor.</li> <li>Give a FreshCart example. Notification kinds × channels (SMS, push, email).</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Bridge vs Adapter? Adapter fits an existing API after the fact; Bridge is designed up front for two hierarchies.</li> <li>Bridge vs Strategy? Strategy swaps one algorithm in one context; Bridge has hierarchies on both sides.</li> <li>Where should retries live? In the abstraction (or one decorator), not in each implementor.</li> <li>How do you keep implementors consistent? A contract test they all pass.</li> <li>How does JDBC find a driver? <code>ServiceLoader</code> registration, then matching the URL prefix.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design a pluggable storage layer for invoices. Primitive implementor, policies in the abstraction, URL-based selection, contract tests, checksums, migration via dual write.</li> <li>How do you handle features only some back ends support? Capability flags or <code>unwrap</code>, not a bigger mandatory interface.</li> <li>How does hexagonal architecture relate to Bridge? Ports are implementor interfaces; adapters are concrete implementors.</li> <li>How would you migrate from one back end to another with no downtime? Dual-write implementor, backfill, compare reads, switch, remove.</li> <li>When is a bridge harmful? Speculative second hierarchies, leaky interfaces, or when it hides performance differences that matter.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>DualWriteBlobStore</code> to <code>InvoiceArchiveDemo</code> for migrations.</p> Solution <pre><code>record DualWriteBlobStore(BlobStore oldStore, BlobStore newStore) implements BlobStore {
    public void put(String k, byte[] d) { oldStore.put(k, d); newStore.put(k, d); }
    public Optional&lt;byte[]&gt; get(String k) { return oldStore.get(k).or(() -&gt; newStore.get(k)); }
    public String name() { return "dual(" + oldStore.name() + "," + newStore.name() + ")"; }
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Chain of Responsibility? Passing a request along handlers until one handles it (or each processes it).</li> <li>Give a Java example. Servlet filters, Spring Security's filter chain.</li> <li>What happens if no handler handles the request? It must be defined: default, error, or queue.</li> <li>How do you stop a filter chain? Don't call <code>chain.doFilter</code> (return a response).</li> <li>Give a FreshCart example. Refund approval: bot, agent, supervisor.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Linked handlers vs list pipeline? Links are GoF; lists are clearer, easier to reorder and test.</li> <li>Why does filter order matter? Later filters depend on what earlier ones prepared (auth, request IDs, forwarded headers).</li> <li>Filter vs HandlerInterceptor? Filters wrap the whole servlet; interceptors wrap controller handling.</li> <li>Chain vs Decorator? Similar shape; chain handlers may stop the request, decorators always delegate.</li> <li>How do you test a chain? Unit-test handlers, then test the assembled chain for order and short-circuits.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design the request pipeline for a public API. Request ID, error mapping, forwarded headers, auth, rate limit, idempotency, validation, with explicit order and metrics.</li> <li>How do you prevent ordering regressions? Named order constants, startup validation, integration tests for security and headers.</li> <li>Design a price-lookup fallback chain. Cache → DB → supplier, timeouts, circuit breakers, metrics, safe terminal behaviour.</li> <li>When is a chain the wrong tool? When routing is a key lookup or all handlers must run (events).</li> <li>How do you debug "who handled this request?" Per-handler metrics and logs with request IDs, security debug logs.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a timing handler to <code>RequestPipelineDemo</code> that records the latency of the rest of the chain.</p> Solution <pre><code>record Timing(Map&lt;String, Long&gt; lastNanos) implements Handler {
    public String name() { return "timing"; }
    public int order() { return 12; }
    public Response handle(Request r, Next next) {
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        try { return next.call(r); }
        finally { lastNanos.put(r.path(), System.nanoTime() - start); }
    }
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does Command do? Turns a request into an object so it can be queued, logged, undone.</li> <li>Name the roles. Command, receiver, invoker, client.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. <code>Runnable</code> submitted to an <code>ExecutorService</code>.</li> <li>How is undo implemented? Each command stores what it changed and implements <code>undo()</code>; a history stack.</li> <li>What's a macro command? A command made of other commands, run in order and undone in reverse.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why clear the redo stack after a new command? Redo of old commands would apply to a different state.</li> <li>What is an idempotency key? A unique ID so a duplicate delivery runs once.</li> <li>Lambda vs class for a command? Lambda for simple in-process; record/class for name, undo, storage.</li> <li>Which errors should be retried? Transient ones (timeouts, 503), with backoff and a limit; not validation errors.</li> <li>Command vs Strategy? Strategy is how to do something; Command is a request to do something, possibly later.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design a reliable refund pipeline. Outbox + queue, idempotency keys end to end, retries with jitter, dead letters, replay tooling, audit.</li> <li>Commands vs events in a microservice architecture? Commands target one handler and can fail; events are facts for many subscribers.</li> <li>How do you get exactly-once effects? Idempotent handlers with keys stored transactionally with the effect.</li> <li>How do you handle undo in distributed workflows? Compensating commands (sagas), not true undo.</li> <li>What do you monitor for a command queue? Oldest message age, throughput, retries, dead letters, handler latency.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add an <code>expiresAt</code> to <code>SendSms</code> in <code>CommandProcessorDemo</code> and drop expired commands.</p> Solution <pre><code>record SendSms(String key, String phone, String text, Instant expiresAt) implements Cmd { }

case SendSms s -&gt; {
    if (clock.instant().isAfter(s.expiresAt())) {
        audit.add(s.key() + " expired, dropped");
        yield "expired";
    }
    yield "sms to " + s.phone();
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What problem does Composite solve? Treating single objects and groups the same way in a tree.</li> <li>Name the three roles. Component, leaf, composite.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. AWT <code>Container</code> holding <code>Component</code>s.</li> <li>How does a composite implement an operation? By calling it on its children and combining the results.</li> <li>Give a FreshCart example. A combo pack containing items and other packs.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Safe vs transparent design? Transparent puts <code>add</code> on every node (runtime errors); safe puts it on composites only (compile-time safety).</li> <li>How do sealed interfaces change Composite? Operations become exhaustive switches outside the nodes; the compiler checks every case.</li> <li>How do you avoid stack overflows? Depth limits and iterative walks with an explicit stack.</li> <li>Composite vs Decorator? Both are recursive; Decorator wraps one object to add behaviour, Composite holds many to treat them as one.</li> <li>How do you prevent cycles? Immutable trees, or a check on <code>add</code> that the new child isn't an ancestor.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How would you store bundle trees in a relational database? Adjacency list with recursive CTEs, materialised paths, or a JSON column for immutable bundles; choose by query pattern.</li> <li>A tree aggregate is slow on a large catalogue. What do you do? Compute once per immutable version, cache, or precompute on write.</li> <li>How do you design an editable tree used by many readers? Persistent immutable trees (path copying) with an atomic root reference.</li> <li>How do you handle trees from untrusted input? Limits on depth and size, streaming parsers, rebuild from trusted data.</li> <li>When is a tree really a graph? When nodes are shared or cyclic; then use graph algorithms with visited sets, not Composite.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>weightGrams()</code> operation to <code>BundlePricingDemo</code> without changing the records.</p> Solution <pre><code>// assuming Sku gets a grams component
static int weightGrams(Line l) {
    return switch (l) {
        case Sku s -&gt; s.grams();
        case Bundle b -&gt; b.lines().stream().mapToInt(BundlePricingDemo::weightGrams).sum();
    };
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Dependency Injection? An object receives its dependencies from outside instead of creating them.</li> <li>Name three injection styles. Constructor, setter, field. Prefer constructor.</li> <li>Why does DI help testing? You can pass fakes through the constructor without global state or frameworks.</li> <li>What is a Spring bean's default scope? Singleton (one per application context).</li> <li>DI vs Dependency Inversion? Inversion is the principle (depend on abstractions); DI is a technique to achieve it.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why is Service Locator considered an anti-pattern? Hidden dependencies, runtime failures, global state that couples tests.</li> <li>What is a composition root? The single place near <code>main</code> where the object graph is built.</li> <li>How do you inject a request-scoped bean into a singleton? A provider (<code>ObjectProvider</code>, <code>Provider&lt;T&gt;</code>) or a scoped proxy.</li> <li>Why did Spring Boot ban circular references? Cycles need half-built objects and signal a design problem.</li> <li>Why might <code>@Transactional</code> not work on a method? Self-invocation or the object was created with <code>new</code>, so no proxy.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Runtime reflection vs compile-time DI: how do you choose? Startup time, native images, compile-time safety vs ecosystem and AOP; serverless favours compile-time.</li> <li>How do you stop test fakes from reaching production? Separate source sets, startup assertions, required profiles, reconciliation as a last line.</li> <li>Design wiring for a modular monolith. One module per bounded context, package-private internals, a public API, a top-level root that composes modules.</li> <li>A service takes 40 s to start. What do you look at? Classpath scanning, eager heavy beans, auto-configuration, <code>/actuator/startup</code>; consider AOT or lazy init with care.</li> <li>When would you not use a DI container? Libraries, CLIs, small Lambdas, and domain modules that should stay framework-free.</li> </ol> <p>Task: In <code>AppContainerDemo</code>, make <code>start()</code> report each cycle only once.</p> Solution <pre><code>private void findCycle(Class&lt;?&gt; t, List&lt;Class&lt;?&gt;&gt; path, List&lt;String&gt; problems, Set&lt;Set&lt;Class&lt;?&gt;&gt;&gt; seen) {
    if (path.contains(t)) {
        List&lt;Class&lt;?&gt;&gt; cycle = path.subList(path.indexOf(t), path.size());
        if (seen.add(new HashSet&lt;&gt;(cycle))) {           // same members = same cycle
            problems.add("cycle: " + cycle.stream().map(Class::getSimpleName).collect(joining(" -&gt; ")) + " -&gt; " + t.getSimpleName());
        }
        return;
    }
    Binding b = bindings.get(t);
    if (b == null) return;
    path.add(t);
    b.deps().forEach(d -&gt; findCycle(d, path, problems, seen));
    path.remove(path.size() - 1);
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Flyweight? Sharing immutable common state across many objects to save memory.</li> <li>Intrinsic vs extrinsic state? Intrinsic is shared and context-free; extrinsic is per use, kept outside or passed in.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. <code>Integer.valueOf</code> cache, String pool.</li> <li>Why must flyweights be immutable? They're shared; a change would affect every user.</li> <li>Why is <code>Integer a = 128, b = 128; a == b</code> false? The cache covers only -128..127.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you build a thread-safe flyweight factory? <code>ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent</code>.</li> <li>Flyweight vs Object Pool? Flyweights are shared at once and immutable; pooled objects are borrowed exclusively.</li> <li>How do you know a flyweight is worth it? Heap histogram, MAT duplicates, before/after measurements.</li> <li>What's G1 string deduplication? GC merges equal strings' backing arrays automatically.</li> <li>Risks of <code>String.intern()</code>? Unbounded growth from high-cardinality or attacker-controlled strings.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>A service needs 6 GB for a catalogue. How do you investigate? Heap dump, dominator tree, duplicate strings; intern low-cardinality attributes; consider off-heap or columnar layouts.</li> <li>How do you protect a shared cache from bad input? Cap distinct values, weak references, metrics and alerts.</li> <li>How will Valhalla value classes change this? Flattened, identity-free small values; less need to share tiny objects.</li> <li>When would you use weak interners? When values come and go and should be reclaimed when unused.</li> <li>How does compressed oops affect the trade-off? References are 4 bytes, so sharing tiny objects saves little.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>MarkerStyles</code> reject unknown combinations from a configuration file instead of creating them.</p> Solution <pre><code>private final Set&lt;String&gt; allowed;    // loaded from config at startup
MarkerStyle get(Status s, Vehicle v) {
    String key = s + "/" + v;
    if (!allowed.contains(key)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("no marker style for " + key);
    return cache.computeIfAbsent(key, k -&gt; createStyle(s, v));
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does Interpreter do? Represents sentences of a small language as a tree and evaluates it.</li> <li>What are terminal and non-terminal expressions? Leaves (numbers, fields) and nodes made of other nodes (and, or, plus).</li> <li>What is the context? The facts the expression is evaluated against.</li> <li>Which pattern does the AST resemble? Composite.</li> <li>Give an example from the JDK. <code>java.util.regex.Pattern</code>.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>What does a parser do that <code>split</code> can't? Handles nesting, precedence and associativity.</li> <li>How does recursive descent encode precedence? Looser levels call tighter levels (<code>expr</code> calls <code>term</code>).</li> <li>How do Java 21 features change the pattern? Sealed records for nodes and exhaustive <code>switch</code> per operation.</li> <li>Why parse once and cache? Parsing dominates cost; ASTs are immutable and shareable.</li> <li>When would you use ANTLR? When the grammar is large, evolving or needs error recovery.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design a promo rule engine for non-developers. Grammar, schema type checking, limits, explain trace, cache, versioned rules, match-rate monitoring.</li> <li>How do you make user expressions safe? Own small grammar, no method calls or loops, size/depth limits, field whitelist; never raw SpEL/OGNL.</li> <li>How would you speed up a hot interpreter? Compile to lambdas, then bytecode; order <code>and</code> chains by selectivity.</li> <li>How would you detect conflicting promotions? Analyse ASTs (interval reasoning on numeric comparisons) or use an SMT solver.</li> <li>Interpreter vs Strategy vs rules engine (Drools)? Strategy picks among coded behaviours; Interpreter runs user-written ones; Drools adds forward chaining over many rules.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add <code>in</code> to <code>SafeRuleEngineDemo</code> so authors can write <code>category in ("fruit", "dairy")</code>.</p> Solution <pre><code>record In(Node field, List&lt;Lit&gt; values) implements Node { }   // add to the permits list

// parser, in cmp(): after primary(), if the token is "in", read "(" lit ("," lit)* ")"
// type checker:
case In i -&gt; {
    Class&lt;?&gt; f = typeOf(i.field(), schema);
    for (Lit v : i.values()) if (v.value().getClass() != f) throw new IllegalArgumentException("mixed types in 'in'");
    yield Boolean.class;
}
// evaluator:
case In i -&gt; { Object v = eval(i.field(), facts, trace); yield i.values().stream().anyMatch(l -&gt; l.value().equals(v)); }
// show():
case In i -&gt; show(i.field()) + " in " + i.values().stream().map(SafeRuleEngineDemo::show).toList();
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does Iterator do? Gives sequential access without exposing the collection's internal structure.</li> <li>Iterator vs Iterable? <code>Iterable</code> creates iterators (enables for-each); <code>Iterator</code> walks one traversal.</li> <li>How do you remove while looping? <code>Iterator.remove()</code> or <code>removeIf</code>.</li> <li>What is <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>? Thrown by a fail-fast iterator when the collection changes during iteration.</li> <li>What should <code>next()</code> do at the end? Throw <code>NoSuchElementException</code>.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Fail-fast vs weakly consistent? Fail-fast throws on change (best effort); weakly consistent never throws, may reflect changes.</li> <li>What is a Spliterator? The iterator behind streams: <code>tryAdvance</code>, <code>trySplit</code>, characteristics.</li> <li>Why is OFFSET pagination risky? Rows shift with inserts/deletes; deep pages are slow.</li> <li>External vs internal iteration? Caller drives (control, early exit) vs collection drives (composition, parallelism).</li> <li>How do you make a lazy paged iterator? Fetch the next page in <code>hasNext()</code> when the current page is exhausted.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design an export of 500 million rows. Keyset pagination, bounded pages, resumable from last ID, checkpoints, metrics, read replica.</li> <li>When do parallel streams hurt? Poorly splitting or I/O-bound sources, tiny workloads, shared common pool contention.</li> <li>How do you design API pagination cursors? Opaque, signed, unique ordered keys, authorisation per page, size limits.</li> <li>Server-side cursor vs keyset pages? Cursors hold connections and transactions open; keyset pages are short, resumable.</li> <li>How do you guarantee an iterator terminates? Strictly increasing unique cursor, advance checks, time and count limits.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>stream()</code> method to <code>DeliveryRoute</code> in <code>IteratorIterableDemo</code>.</p> Solution <pre><code>Stream&lt;Stop&gt; stream() {
    return StreamSupport.stream(
        Spliterators.spliterator(iterator(), size, Spliterator.ORDERED | Spliterator.SIZED | Spliterator.NONNULL),
        false);
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does Mediator do? Centralises interaction between objects so they don't reference each other.</li> <li>What are colleagues? Objects that report to the mediator instead of calling each other.</li> <li>Give an example. A checkout screen coordinating address, slots, payment and button.</li> <li>What problem does it solve? A web of n² references and scattered rules.</li> <li>What's the main risk? A god mediator.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Mediator vs Observer? Observer broadcasts; Mediator coordinates several parties with its own logic.</li> <li>Is an event bus a mediator? Yes, a generic one without business rules.</li> <li>Mediator vs Facade? Facade simplifies a subsystem for clients; Mediator coordinates peers that talk through it.</li> <li>How do you prevent update loops? Don't trigger reports from mediator-driven updates; re-entry guards.</li> <li>Spring events: sync or async? Synchronous by default; use <code>@Async</code> or after-commit listeners for side effects.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Orchestration vs choreography? Orchestrator = mediator (visible flow, central control); choreography = events (loose coupling, hidden flow).</li> <li>How do you split a god mediator? By workflow/bounded context, shared colleagues, events between workflows, module boundaries in CI.</li> <li>Design a command bus for an order service. One handler per command, pipeline behaviours (auth, validation, logging, transactions), startup validation.</li> <li>How do you make in-process events reliable? Publication registry / outbox, after-commit listeners, retries.</li> <li>When is a mediator better modelled as a state machine? When its rules depend on process stage; make states explicit.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a re-entry guard to <code>MediatorColleaguesDemo.CheckoutScreen.changed</code>.</p> Solution <pre><code>private boolean updating;
public void changed(Object widget) {
    if (updating) return;                 // ignore reports caused by our own updates
    updating = true;
    try {
        // ...existing rules...
    } finally {
        updating = false;
    }
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does Memento do? Saves an object's state as a snapshot to restore later, without exposing internals.</li> <li>Name the roles. Originator, memento, caretaker.</li> <li>Give a use case. Undo, discard changes, drafts, checkpoints.</li> <li>Why should mementos be immutable? So the saved state can't change after saving.</li> <li>Who can read a memento's contents? Only the originator.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why are records only shallowly immutable? They hold references; mutable collections inside can still change; copy them.</li> <li>Memento vs Command for undo? Memento stores state; Command stores changes and inverses.</li> <li>How do you bound undo memory? History limits, coalescing, deltas for large state.</li> <li>Why version persisted mementos? Fields change; old drafts must migrate.</li> <li>What can't Memento undo? External side effects (emails, payments).</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design undo for a 50,000-row price editor. Deltas or persistent structures, coalescing, bounded history, publish with diff.</li> <li>Event sourcing vs snapshots? Events are the source of truth; snapshots speed up rebuilding.</li> <li>How do you store user drafts safely? Versioned format, validation, integrity checks, encryption, TTL, deletion on account removal.</li> <li>How do you make a long batch job resumable? Checkpoints (last committed position) in durable storage; idempotent chunks.</li> <li>What are persistent data structures and why use them? Versions share unchanged parts: cheap snapshots, safe concurrent reads.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>UndoHistoryDemo</code> coalesce edits that happen within 1 second into one undo step.</p> Solution <pre><code>private Instant lastSave = Instant.MIN;
void saved(Banner before, Instant now) {
    if (Duration.between(lastSave, now).toMillis() &lt; 1_000 &amp;&amp; !undo.isEmpty()) {
        lastSave = now;            // same burst: keep the earlier snapshot
        return;
    }
    undo.push(before);
    if (undo.size() &gt; limit) undo.removeLast();
    redo.clear();
    lastSave = now;
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is a Null Object? An implementation that does nothing, used instead of <code>null</code>.</li> <li>What does it remove from callers? <code>null</code> checks and the NPEs from forgotten ones.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. <code>Collections.emptyList()</code>, <code>OutputStream.nullOutputStream()</code>.</li> <li>Why make it a singleton? It has no state, so one instance is enough.</li> <li>When should you not use it? When the operation must happen (payment) or callers need to know it's missing.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Null Object vs Optional? Optional: caller decides (return values). Null Object: behaviour already decided (collaborators).</li> <li>What is Special Case? A subclass with situation-specific behaviour (Guest, InvalidCoupon); Null Object is the special case for absence.</li> <li>Where should <code>null</code> become a special case? At the boundary (repository, client).</li> <li>How can a Null Object hide failures? Silent no-ops after misconfiguration; fix with startup logs and counters.</li> <li>What's a good neutral value? The operation's identity element; none exists for min/max.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design wiring for optional integrations. Feature flags choose real vs null at startup, required beans fail fast, disabled list exposed on health, suppression metrics.</li> <li>How do you handle a degraded dependency at checkout? A Special Case returning safe values, flagging degraded, queuing owed work.</li> <li>Null Objects and security? Neutral for access is deny; never a permissive no-op authorizer.</li> <li>How would you eliminate NPEs in a large codebase? JSpecify + NullAway, empties not nulls, boundary conversion, Optional returns.</li> <li>When does Null Object conflict with fail-fast? When it masks configuration errors; decide per collaborator whether it's optional.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add an <code>AuditLog</code> collaborator to <code>CheckoutWiringDemo</code> that is required in production but optional in tests.</p> Solution <pre><code>interface AuditLog { void record(String event); }
enum NoAudit implements AuditLog { INSTANCE; public void record(String e) { } }

// in wire(): required unless explicitly allowed (tests only)
AuditLog audit = (AuditLog) realBeans.get("audit");
if (audit == null) {
    if (!Boolean.parseBoolean(config.getOrDefault("audit.optional", "false")))
        throw new IllegalStateException("audit log is required");
    audit = off(disabled, "audit", NoAudit.INSTANCE);
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is an object pool? A set of reusable expensive objects lent out and returned.</li> <li>Give two Java examples. HikariCP (connections) and <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> (threads).</li> <li>Why return in <code>finally</code>? So objects are returned even when exceptions happen; otherwise the pool leaks.</li> <li>What should happen when the pool is empty? Wait up to a timeout, then fail clearly.</li> <li>Should you pool <code>StringBuilder</code>s? No; allocation is cheap and pooling adds bugs.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>What is <code>maxLifetime</code> for? Retire connections before the database or firewall closes them.</li> <li>How do you find a connection leak? <code>leakDetectionThreshold</code> stack traces, pool metrics, hold-time timers.</li> <li>Why bounded queues in thread pools? Back-pressure and bounded memory under overload.</li> <li>How do virtual threads change pooling? Don't pool threads; limit the resource with semaphores or its pool.</li> <li>How big should a connection pool be? Based on DB capacity and hold time, often small (tens), across all instances.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>The pool is exhausted in production. Walk through your response. Check hold times and slow queries, find leaks, shed load; don't just raise the size.</li> <li>Design connection management for an autoscaling service. Per-instance pools sized from a global budget, a connection proxy, timeouts, bulkheads.</li> <li>How do you protect a partner API with a rate limit across instances? Distributed rate limiter or token bucket service, local semaphores as a first line.</li> <li>When would you write a custom pool? For native or licensed resources with no library support, with leases, validation, eviction and metrics.</li> <li>Now 10x traffic with virtual threads; the DB is the bottleneck. What changes? Caching, read replicas, query optimisation, admission control; not more connections.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>ConnectionPoolDemo</code>'s pool report average hold time.</p> Solution <pre><code>private final Map&lt;Connection, Long&gt; borrowedAt = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();
private final LongAdder totalHoldNanos = new LongAdder(), returns = new LongAdder();

Connection getConnection(long timeoutMs) throws InterruptedException {
    Connection c = free.poll(timeoutMs, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
    if (c == null) throw new IllegalStateException("timed out");
    borrowedAt.put(c, System.nanoTime());
    return c;
}
void close(Connection c) {
    totalHoldNanos.add(System.nanoTime() - borrowedAt.remove(c));
    returns.increment();
    free.add(c);
}
double averageHoldMillis() { return returns.sum() == 0 ? 0 : totalHoldNanos.sum() / 1e6 / returns.sum(); }
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is Prototype? Create objects by copying a configured instance and customising the copy.</li> <li>Shallow vs deep copy? Shallow shares inner objects; deep duplicates mutable inner objects.</li> <li>Why avoid <code>Cloneable</code>? Shallow, skips constructors, breaks <code>final</code> fields, awkward exception.</li> <li>What's the alternative? Copy constructors or copy factories, or immutable records with withers.</li> <li>What's a prototype registry? Named templates that hand out copies.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Which fields should a copy not copy? Identity, secrets and operational state.</li> <li>Why copy on registration in a registry? So later edits to the source don't change the template.</li> <li>Is <code>array.clone()</code> OK? Yes for arrays, but it's shallow for arrays of objects or arrays of arrays.</li> <li>How do immutable objects change the picture? They can be shared; copying is only needed for mutable parts.</li> <li>Prototype vs Builder's <code>toBuilder()</code>? Both start from an existing object; <code>toBuilder</code> suits many changes with validation at build.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design store onboarding from templates. Versioned templates, explicit copy semantics, secret exclusion, go-live validation, audit trail.</li> <li>How do you propagate template fixes to existing copies? Track template version per copy, compute diffs, migrate selectively with approvals.</li> <li>When are persistent data structures worth it? Frequent copies of large structures with small edits.</li> <li>How do you test copy semantics across a codebase? Property-style tests: mutate the copy, assert the source unchanged, for every copyable type.</li> <li>Now 10x stores per month: what breaks? Manual go-live checks and template drift; automate validation and template governance.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>DiscountRule</code> in <code>CampaignCloneDemo</code> immutable so <code>Campaign</code> can copy its rule list shallowly.</p> Solution <p><pre><code>record DiscountRule(String category, int percent, long minBasketCents) {
    DiscountRule withPercent(int p) { return new DiscountRule(category, p, minBasketCents); }
}
// Campaign constructor: this.rules = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(rules);   // shallow is now safe
// Mumbai: mumbai.rules.set(0, mumbai.rules.get(0).withPercent(20));
</code></pre> Immutable elements remove the need for a deep copy.</p>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is a Proxy? A stand-in with the same interface that controls access to the real object.</li> <li>Name four kinds. Virtual, protection, caching, remote.</li> <li>Proxy vs Decorator? Same structure; Proxy controls access, Decorator adds behaviour.</li> <li>Give a JDK example. <code>java.lang.reflect.Proxy</code>; also <code>Collections.unmodifiableList</code>.</li> <li>Give a Spring example. <code>@Transactional</code> works through a proxy around your bean.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>JDK dynamic proxy vs CGLIB? JDK needs interfaces; CGLIB/ByteBuddy subclass classes and can't intercept <code>final</code> methods.</li> <li>Why doesn't <code>@Transactional</code> work on an internal call? Self-invocation bypasses the proxy.</li> <li>Why rethrow <code>getCause()</code> in an <code>InvocationHandler</code>? <code>Method.invoke</code> wraps exceptions in <code>InvocationTargetException</code>.</li> <li>What causes <code>LazyInitializationException</code>? A Hibernate lazy proxy accessed after the session closed.</li> <li>How do you keep a caching proxy correct per user? Put the user or tenant in the key and keep cached methods pure.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Proxy-based AOP vs AspectJ weaving: trade-offs? Proxies are simple but miss self-calls, private, final, <code>new</code>; weaving covers them with build complexity.</li> <li>Design a client for a remote pricing service. Typed interface, batch methods, deadlines, retries with budgets, circuit breaker, cache with TTL.</li> <li>How do proxies affect GraalVM native images? No runtime class generation: AOT-generated proxies or reflection/proxy hints.</li> <li>A security annotation was bypassed in production. How? Self-invocation, direct <code>new</code>, a non-proxied bean, or an internal endpoint; defence in depth.</li> <li>When would you hand-write a proxy instead of using AOP? When the concern is specific, performance-sensitive, or must be visible and explicit in code review.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>PartnerPriceCacheDemo</code>'s proxy evict the oldest entry when it holds more than 1,000 SKUs.</p> Solution <pre><code>private final Map&lt;String, Entry&gt; cache = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;(16, 0.75f, true) {
    @Override protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry&lt;String, Entry&gt; eldest) {
        return size() &gt; 1_000;              // LRU eviction
    }
};
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is a Specification? A business rule as an object answering <code>isSatisfiedBy(candidate)</code>.</li> <li>Why not a finder method per filter? Combinations explode; specs combine freely.</li> <li>How do you combine specs? <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code>.</li> <li>What JDK type is a simple spec? <code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li> <li>Give an example. Free delivery: total ≥ 499 or first order, and within 5 km.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How does Spring Data JPA use specifications? <code>toPredicate(root, query, cb)</code> builds a criteria predicate; <code>JpaSpecificationExecutor.findAll(spec)</code>.</li> <li>Why not filter in memory with Predicate? It loads every row; push filters to the database.</li> <li>What can a custom spec type do that a lambda can't? Describe, explain, translate, serialise.</li> <li>How do you prevent SQL injection in specs? Bind parameters, whitelist fields.</li> <li>What's an empty AllOf? TRUE (matches everything).</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design a product search API with dynamic filters. Whitelist and validate, build a spec tree, render to SQL/search with binds, paging, mandatory visibility rule, index the common filters.</li> <li>How do you keep in-memory and SQL interpretations consistent? Define semantics once; fixture tests comparing both (Testcontainers).</li> <li>How would you implement "add Rs 120 for free delivery"? Partially satisfied specs returning the failing parts and the gap.</li> <li>Specification vs Querydsl vs Criteria API? Specification is the pattern; Criteria and Querydsl are query builders a spec can use.</li> <li>How do you stop callers bypassing tenant filters? The repository adds them; don't expose raw <code>findAll(spec)</code>.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add <code>min_rating</code> (1 to 5) to <code>ProductSearchSpecDemo</code>.</p> Solution <pre><code>record Product(String sku, String name, String category, long pricePaise, boolean organic, int stock, int rating) { }
record AtLeast(String field, int min) implements Spec { }    // add to the permits list

// fromRequest: add "min_rating" to the whitelist, then
if (q.containsKey("min_rating")) {
    int r = Integer.parseInt(q.get("min_rating"));
    if (r &lt; 1 || r &gt; 5) throw new IllegalArgumentException("min_rating must be 1 to 5");
    parts.add(new AtLeast("rating", r));
}
// toSql:  case AtLeast a -&gt; a.field() + " &gt;= ?" + bind(params, a.min());
// test:   case AtLeast a -&gt; p.rating() &gt;= a.min();
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does the State pattern do? Lets an object change behaviour when its state changes by delegating to state objects.</li> <li>Name the parts. Context, state interface, concrete states.</li> <li>How would you model order statuses simply? An enum with allowed transitions.</li> <li>What's an illegal transition? An event not allowed in the current state (deliver a cancelled order).</li> <li>Why not booleans for status? Flags combine into impossible states.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>State vs Strategy? Strategy is chosen by the client and stable; State changes itself with events.</li> <li>How do sealed types improve State? Each state carries only its own data; switches are exhaustive.</li> <li>How do you persist an enum state safely? <code>EnumType.STRING</code> plus a version column.</li> <li>What's a guard? A condition that must hold for a transition (rider assigned before dispatch).</li> <li>Why make event handling idempotent? Events are redelivered; duplicates must not repeat effects.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Two services update the same order concurrently. Design it. Optimistic versioning, events not states, idempotency, outbox side effects, single-writer per order (partitioning).</li> <li>When would you use a workflow engine? Long-running, cross-service flows with timeouts, retries and compensations.</li> <li>How does event sourcing relate to State? State is a fold of events through the transition function.</li> <li>How do you evolve a state machine in production? Add states backward-compatibly, migrate data, handle in-flight instances, version the machine.</li> <li>How do you test a state machine exhaustively? Every (state, event) pair, property-based tests over random event sequences, invariants.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>RETURNED</code> state reachable from <code>DELIVERED</code> within 2 days, to <code>OrderStateMachineDemo</code>.</p> Solution <pre><code>enum S { PLACED, PACKED, OUT_FOR_DELIVERY, DELIVERED, CANCELLED, RETURNED }
enum E { PACK, DISPATCH, DELIVER, CANCEL, RETURN }

new Transition(S.DELIVERED, E.RETURN, S.RETURNED,
        e -&gt; Duration.between(deliveredAt(e), clock.get()).toDays() &lt; 2,
        "returns are accepted within 2 days of delivery")
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Name the five test doubles. Dummy, stub, fake, spy, mock.</li> <li>Stub vs mock? A stub returns canned answers; a mock verifies expected calls.</li> <li>What's Arrange-Act-Assert? Set up, act once, check the outcome.</li> <li>How do you test code that uses the current time? Inject <code>Clock</code>, use <code>Clock.fixed</code>.</li> <li>What's a Test Data Builder? Defaults plus fluent overrides for test objects.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>What's a fake, and why is it risky? A working in-memory implementation; it can drift from the real thing.</li> <li>What is a seam? A place to change behaviour without editing there (injection, strategy, decorator).</li> <li>Why "don't mock what you don't own"? Your mock encodes your assumptions about their API; wrap and fake your interface.</li> <li>How do you write contract tests in JUnit 5? Abstract test class + subclass per implementation, often <code>@Nested</code>.</li> <li>State vs interaction testing? State survives refactoring; interactions check outgoing effects.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design the test strategy for a payment integration. Port interface, verified fake with contract tests, sandbox nightly, idempotency and failure modes, Testcontainers for DB.</li> <li>How do you keep a large test suite fast and reliable? Pyramid, fakes over mocks, no shared state, parallel execution, flake tracking.</li> <li>London vs Chicago TDD? Mocks to design roles vs real objects and state; mix at boundaries.</li> <li>How do you know your tests are good? Mutation testing, coverage of failure paths, contracts on fakes.</li> <li>Consumer-driven contracts vs provider contract tests? Pact: consumers publish expectations the provider verifies; provider suites verify implementations of one interface.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a "slow" mode to <code>FakePaymentGateway</code> and a contract test that a timeout is reported as <code>GatewayTimeout</code>, not as a decline.</p> Solution <pre><code>// in FakePaymentGateway
private boolean timeOutNext;
public FakePaymentGateway timeOutNextCall() { timeOutNext = true; return this; }

public Result charge(String key, String card, long paise) {
    if (timeOutNext || card.equals(TIMEOUT_CARD)) { timeOutNext = false; throw new GatewayTimeout(); }
    ...
}

// contract (works for the sandbox too, via the magic timeout card)
@Test
void timeoutIsNotADecline() {
    assertThrows(VerifiedFakeDemo.GatewayTimeout.class,
            () -&gt; gateway.charge("k6", "4000000000000119", 100));
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does Visitor do? Adds new operations to element types without changing them.</li> <li>What's <code>accept</code> for? It calls the visitor method for the element's own type (double dispatch).</li> <li>Why doesn't overloading solve it? Overloads are chosen by compile-time types.</li> <li>Give an example. GST and shipping weight visitors over cart items.</li> <li>How does Java 21 simplify Visitor? Sealed interface + exhaustive <code>switch</code> with pattern matching.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>What's double dispatch? Selecting code by two runtime types via two virtual calls.</li> <li>What is the expression problem? Easy to add types or operations, not both.</li> <li>Why avoid <code>default</code> in switches over sealed types? It disables exhaustiveness checks.</li> <li>When is Visitor a bad choice? When element types change often.</li> <li>What's an acyclic visitor? Per-type visitor interfaces; no dependency cycle; visitors handle a subset.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design a validation framework for a product tree. Path-aware walker collecting all problems, sealed node types, severity levels, depth limits.</li> <li>How would you structure analyses for a rules AST? Sealed nodes, folds for evaluation/printing/optimisation, immutable trees.</li> <li>How do sealed types behave across module versions? New subtypes from a library can cause <code>MatchException</code> in old compiled switches; version APIs carefully.</li> <li>When choose a plugin registry over Visitor? When third parties add types and completeness can't be checked.</li> <li>How do you compute several results in one tree walk? A fold returning a record of results (product algebra).</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>DeliveryTip</code> line to <code>InvoiceVisitorDemo</code> with 0% GST.</p> Solution <pre><code>sealed interface Line permits Loose, Packaged, Subscription, GiftCard, DeliveryTip { }
record DeliveryTip(long paise) implements Line { }

// the compiler now flags net, gst and csv; add a case to each:
case DeliveryTip t -&gt; t.paise();          // net
case DeliveryTip t -&gt; 0;                  // gst
case DeliveryTip t -&gt; "tip";              // csv kind
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's an anti-pattern? A common solution that causes more problems than it solves.</li> <li>What's a code smell? A surface symptom that may indicate a design problem.</li> <li>What's a God Object? A class with too many responsibilities.</li> <li>Why are magic numbers bad? Unclear meaning, easy to change inconsistently.</li> <li>Why delete dead code? It misleads readers and adds maintenance; git remembers it.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>What's an anaemic domain model? Entities without behaviour; rules duplicated in services.</li> <li>How do you fix a repeated switch on type? Polymorphism: enum with behaviour, Strategy, or sealed types.</li> <li>Why is Singleton overuse a problem? Hidden dependencies, global state, hard to test.</li> <li>What's a Golden Hammer? Using a favourite tool for everything.</li> <li>How do you refactor safely? Characterization tests, small steps, behaviour-preserving commits.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you decide which debt to pay first? Churn × complexity hotspots, incident data, upcoming features.</li> <li>How do you introduce quality gates in a legacy code base? Baseline existing issues, gate only new code, shrink the baseline.</li> <li>Rewrite or refactor a God Object? Refactor incrementally (strangler fig), rarely rewrite.</li> <li>When is a "smell" actually fine? Stable code, intentional trade-offs (DTOs are anaemic by design).</li> <li>How do anti-patterns relate to team structure? Conway's Law: ownership boundaries shape code boundaries.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a "Feature Envy" rule to <code>SmellScannerDemo</code>: flag a method whose body calls getters on one other object more than 4 times (e.g., <code>order.getX()</code>).</p> Solution <pre><code>private static final Pattern GETTER_CALL = Pattern.compile("\\b(\\w+)\\.get\\w+\\(\\)");

static void featureEnvy(String file, String name, String body, List&lt;Finding&gt; out) {
    Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; calls = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();
    Matcher m = GETTER_CALL.matcher(body);
    while (m.find()) calls.merge(m.group(1), 1, Integer::sum);
    calls.forEach((target, n) -&gt; {
        if (n &gt; 4) out.add(new Finding(file, "FeatureEnvy", Severity.MAJOR, name + " calls " + n + " getters on " + target));
    });
}
// call it from scan() with the method's body lines (start .. start + length)
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Why are immutable objects thread-safe? Their state never changes after construction.</li> <li>What's a thread pool for? Reusing a bounded set of threads for many tasks.</li> <li>What does a <code>BlockingQueue</code> do when full? <code>put</code> blocks; <code>offer</code> returns false (or waits with timeout).</li> <li>Why is <code>count++</code> unsafe? It's read-modify-write, not atomic.</li> <li>What's a virtual thread? A lightweight JVM-scheduled thread for blocking I/O.</li> </ol> <ol> <li><code>synchronized</code> vs <code>ReentrantLock</code>? Lock adds tryLock, timeouts, fairness, multiple conditions.</li> <li>What's happens-before? The visibility/ordering guarantee between actions (locks, volatile, start/join).</li> <li>Balking vs guarded suspension? Return immediately vs wait until the condition holds.</li> <li>Why bound queues? To apply back-pressure and avoid OOM under overload.</li> <li>When not to use virtual threads? CPU-bound work; don't pool them.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design slot booking for a 9 pm rush. Atomic claim, DB constraint, idempotent request IDs, semaphore bulkhead, timeouts, load tests.</li> <li>How do you find a deadlock in production? Thread dump (jstack/jcmd), look for lock cycles; fix lock ordering or use tryLock.</li> <li>How do you size pools? Little's Law; cores for CPU work; dependency limits for I/O.</li> <li>What's pinning and how is it fixed? Virtual thread stuck to its carrier while blocking in <code>synchronized</code>; use ReentrantLock or Java 24+.</li> <li>ThreadLocal vs ScopedValue? Mutable, manual cleanup vs immutable, bounded lifetime, cheap for virtual threads.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>SlotBookingServiceDemo</code> let customers cancel, freeing the seat and letting a retried booking for the same request ID succeed again.</p> Solution <pre><code>boolean cancel(String requestId) {
    CompletableFuture&lt;Result&gt; f = byRequestId.get(requestId);
    if (f == null || !(f.join() instanceof Booked b)) return false;
    if (!byRequestId.remove(requestId, f)) return false;     // only one cancel wins
    slots.get(b.slot()).release();
    publish();
    return true;
}
// test: book, cancel twice concurrently -&gt; exactly one returns true, seat count restored once
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's a Repository? Collection-like access to domain objects that hides storage.</li> <li>What's a DTO? An object shaped for a boundary, carrying only needed data.</li> <li>What does a Service Layer do? Defines use cases and owns transactions.</li> <li>Active Record vs Data Mapper? Object saves itself vs separate mapper.</li> <li>What's lazy loading? Loading related data on first access.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>What's the N+1 problem and how do you fix it? 1 query + 1 per row; fix with join fetch, entity graphs, batch fetching, projections.</li> <li>What's an Identity Map? One object per row per unit of work; JPA's persistence context.</li> <li>How does JPA know what to update? Dirty checking against snapshots at flush.</li> <li>Why not return entities from controllers? Lazy-loading errors, leaked fields, coupled API.</li> <li>Transaction Script vs Domain Model? Procedures for simple logic vs objects with rules for complex logic.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Two users edit the same order; what happens and how do you handle it? Lost update; use <code>@Version</code> and show a conflict, or merge.</li> <li>When would you bypass the ORM? Reports, bulk updates, hot paths: jOOQ/JDBC or atomic SQL.</li> <li>How do you keep side effects consistent with the transaction? Outbox or after-commit events.</li> <li>How big should a unit of work be? One use case, one aggregate where possible; short-lived.</li> <li>Is <code>open-in-view</code> good? Convenient but hides N+1 and holds connections; disable it in APIs.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add delete support to <code>OrderPersistenceDemo</code>'s unit of work: <code>remove(Shipment)</code> should produce <code>DELETE ... WHERE version = ?</code> at commit and fail on a stale version.</p> Solution <pre><code>private final Set&lt;String&gt; removed = new LinkedHashSet&lt;&gt;();
void remove(Shipment s) {
    if (created.remove(s)) { identityMap.remove(s.id()); return; }   // never written: nothing to delete
    removed.add(s.id());
}
// in commit(): pass deletes to the batch, built from snapshots so the version is the loaded one
List&lt;Row&gt; deletes = removed.stream().map(snapshots::get).toList();
// Database.executeBatch checks versions for updates and deletes before applying anything,
// then: deletes.forEach(r -&gt; { shipments.remove(r.id()); log.add("DELETE " + r.id() + " WHERE version=" + r.version()); });
// and commit() must skip removed ids when computing updates.
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Checked vs unchecked? Checked must be declared or caught; unchecked (<code>RuntimeException</code>) needn't be.</li> <li>What's try-with-resources? Automatically closes resources, even on exceptions.</li> <li>Why not <code>catch (Exception e) {}</code>? It hides failures; bugs become silent.</li> <li>What's exception chaining? Wrapping with the original as cause.</li> <li>What's fail fast? Stop early with a clear error when something is invalid.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>When would you use a Result type instead of exceptions? For expected business outcomes that callers must handle.</li> <li>What's exception translation? Rethrowing in the current layer's vocabulary, keeping the cause.</li> <li>How do you validate a form? Notification pattern: collect all errors, return them together.</li> <li>Which errors should be retried? Transient ones (timeouts, 429, 5xx) on idempotent operations.</li> <li>Where should you log exceptions? Once, where handled.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design error handling for a payments service. Classify retryable, idempotency keys, translate gateway errors, customer vs log messages, metrics by code.</li> <li>Exceptions or Results in a new code base? Results in the domain, exceptions for infrastructure, translate at boundaries; be consistent.</li> <li>A batch job sometimes corrupts data on bad input; how do you redesign it? Per-row results, error budget, apply after decision, sanity checks, replayable rejects.</li> <li>How do you expose errors in a public API? RFC 9457 problem details, stable codes, correlation IDs, no internals.</li> <li>What's the cost of exceptions? Stack-trace capture; avoid for control flow, or disable stack traces for hot expected errors.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a sanity check to <code>SupplierImportDemo</code>: reject (code <code>E_PRICE_JUMP</code>) any row whose new price differs from the current catalogue price by more than 50%.</p> Solution <pre><code>static RowResult checkJump(RowResult r, int line, Map&lt;String, Long&gt; catalogue) {
    if (!(r instanceof Good g)) return r;
    Long old = catalogue.get(g.price().sku());
    if (old == null) return r;
    long diff = Math.abs(g.price().paise() - old);
    return diff * 2 &gt; old
            ? new Bad(List.of(new RowError(line, "E_PRICE_JUMP", old + " -&gt; " + g.price().paise())))
            : r;
}
// in runImport: switch (checkJump(parseRow(line, text, cfg), line, catalogue)) { ... }
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is a pure function? Same input gives same output, no side effects.</li> <li>What is a higher-order function? One that takes or returns a function.</li> <li><code>map</code> vs <code>flatMap</code> on Optional? <code>flatMap</code> is for a function that already returns Optional.</li> <li><code>orElse</code> vs <code>orElseGet</code>? <code>orElse</code> always evaluates its argument; <code>orElseGet</code> is lazy.</li> <li>What does <code>andThen</code> do? Runs one function then another on its result.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why are immutable objects thread-safe? Nothing changes after construction, so no races.</li> <li>What's a Result/Either type for? Expected failures as values, chained with <code>flatMap</code>.</li> <li>When are parallel streams a bad idea? Small inputs, blocking I/O, shared mutable state, or order-dependent side effects.</li> <li><code>unmodifiableList</code> vs <code>List.copyOf</code>? View vs snapshot.</li> <li>What's partial application? Fixing some arguments to get a function of the rest.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Explain functional core, imperative shell. Pure logic inside, I/O at the edges; core unit-tested, shell integration-tested.</li> <li>Result vs Validation? First error (monadic) vs all errors (applicative).</li> <li>How do persistent collections stay efficient? Structural sharing (tries, cons lists).</li> <li>How would you make a batch job deterministic? Pure functions, exact arithmetic, sorted output, shuffled-input tests.</li> <li>Where would you not use functional style in Java? Hot numeric loops, heavy I/O orchestration, or where it hurts readability.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a "peak hour bonus" (Rs 10 for deliveries marked <code>peak</code>) to <code>RiderSettlementDemo</code> without breaking determinism.</p> Solution <pre><code>record Delivery(String riderId, String zone, double km, boolean late, boolean peak) { }

// parse: accept a 5th field "peak" / "off_peak"
static BigDecimal earning(Delivery d) {
    Rate r = rate(d.zone());
    BigDecimal gross = r.base().add(r.perKm().multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(d.km())));
    BigDecimal adjusted = d.late() ? gross.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.90")) : gross;
    BigDecimal bonus = d.peak() ? BigDecimal.TEN : BigDecimal.ZERO;
    return adjusted.add(bonus).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);
}
// the shuffled-input test keeps passing because earning() is still pure
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What does a record generate? Constructor, accessors, <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code>, <code>toString</code>.</li> <li>What does <code>sealed</code> do? Restricts which classes can implement the type.</li> <li>How does a lambda replace Strategy? The strategy interface has one method, so a lambda is the strategy.</li> <li>Best way to write a singleton? A single-constant enum, or let DI manage it.</li> <li>Where do you validate a record? In the compact constructor.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How does a pattern switch replace Visitor? Type and record patterns over a sealed type, exhaustive, no <code>accept</code>.</li> <li>Why avoid <code>default</code> in such switches? It disables the completeness check.</li> <li>Are records immutable? Shallowly; copy mutable components.</li> <li>Can JPA entities be records? No; they need mutability and identity equality. Records suit DTOs and projections.</li> <li>What is a guard? <code>case X x when condition</code> adds a condition to a pattern.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>When choose sealed + switch vs polymorphism? Stable variants, growing operations vs the opposite (expression problem).</li> <li>What is <code>MatchException</code>? Thrown when a switch meets a subtype unknown at compile time (separate compilation).</li> <li>How would you migrate a legacy visitor-based codebase? Seal the hierarchy, convert visitors to switch functions one at a time, records for nodes, tests per permitted subclass.</li> <li>Risks of records in APIs? Component changes break the canonical constructor and serialisation; toString leaks.</li> <li>How do these features support "make illegal states unrepresentable"? Variants carry exactly their data; compiler enforces handling.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>Bnpl(String provider, int installments, long paise)</code> payment to <code>SealedHierarchyDemo</code> with a 3% fee.</p> Solution <pre><code>sealed interface Payment permits Upi, Card, CashOnDelivery, Bnpl { long paise(); }
record Bnpl(String provider, int installments, long paise) implements Payment { }

// the compiler now rejects feePaise until this case is added:
case Bnpl b -&gt; b.paise() * 3 / 100;
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What is IoC? The container creates and wires objects, instead of objects doing it themselves.</li> <li>Default bean scope? Singleton per container.</li> <li>Which pattern is <code>JdbcTemplate</code>? Template Method with callbacks.</li> <li>How does <code>@Transactional</code> work? A proxy starts and ends the transaction around the method.</li> <li>How do you get all beans of an interface? Inject a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> (or <code>Map&lt;String, T&gt;</code>).</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why doesn't <code>@Transactional</code> work on self-calls? The call bypasses the proxy.</li> <li>JDK proxy vs CGLIB? Interfaces vs subclass; CGLIB can't proxy <code>final</code>.</li> <li>Which exceptions roll back by default? Unchecked (<code>RuntimeException</code>, <code>Error</code>).</li> <li>What's <code>@TransactionalEventListener</code> for? Running listeners after commit (or other phases).</li> <li>What does <code>&amp;</code> mean in <code>getBean("&amp;name")</code>? Return the FactoryBean itself.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How would you guarantee notifications for committed orders only, even across restarts? Transactional outbox + async publisher; <code>AFTER_COMMIT</code> alone isn't durable.</li> <li>How does Spring create proxies internally? <code>BeanPostProcessor</code> auto-proxy creators with advisors and pointcuts.</li> <li>Prototype in singleton: how to fix? <code>ObjectProvider</code>, lookup method injection, or scoped proxies.</li> <li>How do you speed up Spring startup? AOT/native, lazy init, fewer auto-configs, <code>proxyBeanMethods = false</code>.</li> <li>When avoid events? When the core flow depends on the result or ordering must be explicit.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Fix <code>ProxiesAopDemo.InventoryService.reserveAllViaSelfCall</code> so it runs in a transaction.</p> Solution <pre><code>// Option 1: make the outer method the boundary
@Transactional
public void reserveAllViaSelfCall(String... skus) { reserveAll(skus); }

// Option 2: call through the proxy via another bean
public static class ReservationFacade {
    private final InventoryService inventory;   // the injected proxy
    public ReservationFacade(InventoryService inventory) { this.inventory = inventory; }
    public void reserve(String... skus) { inventory.reserveAll(skus); }
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Name a Decorator in the JDK. <code>BufferedReader</code>, <code>BufferedInputStream</code>, <code>GZIPInputStream</code>.</li> <li>Which JDK type is Strategy? <code>Comparator</code>.</li> <li>What pattern is <code>Iterator</code>? Iterator: traverse without exposing the structure.</li> <li>Is <code>Runtime.getRuntime()</code> a singleton? Yes.</li> <li>What does <code>Arrays.asList</code> return? A fixed-size adapter over the array.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why does <code>Integer a = 128, b = 128; a == b</code> give false? The cache covers −128..127.</li> <li>What's <code>InputStreamReader</code>'s role? An Adapter from bytes to characters.</li> <li>View vs copy? Views reflect source changes; copies don't.</li> <li>How does <code>ServiceLoader</code> find providers? <code>META-INF/services</code> files or <code>provides</code> in module-info.</li> <li>What does <code>Flow</code> add to Observer? Back-pressure via <code>request(n)</code>, plus completion and errors.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design a plugin system without a framework. ServiceLoader, annotation metadata, <code>stream()</code> for lazy instantiation, priority strategy, startup validation, proxy for instrumentation.</li> <li>Why is <code>Observable</code> deprecated? Weak model (no ordering, not serializable, not thread-safe, class not interface); replaced by <code>Flow</code> and listeners.</li> <li>Risks of <code>ServiceLoader</code> in app servers? Class loader visibility and malicious providers.</li> <li>How do value-based classes affect your code? No <code>==</code> or synchronisation on them; future Valhalla changes.</li> <li>When is a dynamic proxy too slow? In hot, CPU-bound paths; use generated code or direct wrappers.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Write a <code>LimitedInputStream</code> decorator that throws after N bytes, to protect against zip bombs.</p> Solution <pre><code>static final class LimitedInputStream extends FilterInputStream {
    private final long max; private long read;
    LimitedInputStream(InputStream in, long max) { super(in); this.max = max; }
    @Override public int read() throws IOException {
        int b = super.read();
        if (b &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; ++read &gt; max) throw new IOException("input larger than " + max + " bytes");
        return b;
    }
    @Override public int read(byte[] b, int off, int len) throws IOException {
        int n = super.read(b, off, len);
        if (n &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; (read += n) &gt; max) throw new IOException("input larger than " + max + " bytes");
        return n;
    }
}
// usage: new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(new LimitedInputStream(new GZIPInputStream(upload), 50_000_000), UTF_8))
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Are interface calls slow in Java? Usually no: the JIT inlines them.</li> <li>What's boxing? Wrapping a primitive in an object (<code>long</code> → <code>Long</code>).</li> <li>Why benchmark with JMH? It handles warm-up, dead-code elimination, forks and statistics.</li> <li>What's JIT compilation? Compiling hot bytecode to machine code at runtime.</li> <li>Does DI slow down startup? Reflective DI can; compile-time DI avoids it.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>What's a megamorphic call site? One that sees 3+ receiver types: no inlining.</li> <li>What's escape analysis? Removing allocations of objects that don't escape a compiled method.</li> <li>Why can a benchmark show impossible numbers? Dead-code elimination or constant folding.</li> <li>Proxy vs MethodHandle vs reflection cost? Reflection slowest; MethodHandle near direct when constant.</li> <li>When does pattern overhead matter? Tiny methods in hot loops, not around I/O.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>A team wants to remove all interfaces for speed; how do you respond? Measure with JMH/JFR; show inlining; optimise only hot spots.</li> <li>How do you reduce startup for a Spring service on serverless? AOT/native image, CDS/Leyden, CRaC/SnapStart, fewer beans.</li> <li>How do you design performance regression tests in CI? JMH on stable runners, relative budgets, multiple forks, noise handling.</li> <li>Why can a microbenchmark mislead about production? Profile pollution, different data shapes, GC and cache effects.</li> <li>How would Valhalla change pattern costs? Value objects flattened; no allocation for stored values.</li> </ol> <p>Task: In <code>PricingHotPathDemo</code>, keep four chain shapes but make each call site monomorphic again, without removing the Decorator pattern.</p> Solution <pre><code>// Process each shape in its own loop method so each has its own call-site profile.
static long runShape0(Price p, long[] lines) { long s = 0; for (long l : lines) s += p.of(l); return s; }
static long runShape1(Price p, long[] lines) { long s = 0; for (long l : lines) s += p.of(l); return s; }
// ... one per shape, or group input lines by shape and dispatch once per batch:
long total = 0;
for (Price p : variants) total += batchFor(p).run(p, lines);   // batchFor returns a dedicated loop per shape
// Re-measure: each loop is monomorphic, so the chain inlines again.
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's refactoring? Changing structure without changing behaviour.</li> <li>Why tests before refactoring? To detect accidental behaviour changes.</li> <li>What's Extract Method? Moving a block into a named method.</li> <li>What's a creation method? A named static factory like <code>List.of</code>.</li> <li>Why small steps? Easy to verify and revert.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>When do you replace a conditional with polymorphism? When the same switch appears in several places.</li> <li>What's a golden master? Recorded current outputs used as expected results.</li> <li>How do you extract a strategy? Extract each branch, introduce the interface, replace the conditional with a lookup.</li> <li>When would you remove a pattern? When it has one implementation and no variation.</li> <li>Why separate refactoring from feature commits? Reviewability and safe reverts.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you replace a critical component in production? Parallel change: expand, shadow, migrate by percentage, contract; kill switch.</li> <li>Polymorphism vs sealed + switch? Types change often → polymorphism; operations change often → sealed + switch.</li> <li>How do you refactor a 5,000-line class nobody understands? Characterization tests from real inputs, hotspot first, extract by responsibility, Mikado.</li> <li>What can go wrong with shadow traffic? Duplicate side effects, extra load, data exposure in logs.</li> <li>How do you refactor across 200 repositories? Automated recipes (OpenRewrite), staged rollout, CI verification.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a "Diwali 15% on sweets" rule to <code>DiscountRulesRefactorDemo</code> without editing any existing rule.</p> Solution <pre><code>record FestiveSweets(int sweetsPaise) implements DiscountRule {
    public String name() { return "Diwali 15% on sweets"; }
    public long discount(Cart c) { return sweetsPaise * 15L / 100; }
}
// Cart would carry the sweets subtotal; in practice the rule reads cart lines by category.
var engine = new DiscountEngine(List.of(new FirstOrder(), new MemberFivePercent(),
        new DairyBundle(), new Fresh10Coupon(), new FestiveSweets(40_000)), 50);
// existing rules and the engine are unchanged: Open/Closed
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's MVC? Model (data), View (display), Controller (input handling).</li> <li>Why separate UI from logic? Testability and reuse.</li> <li>What does a ViewModel do? Holds screen state and actions; survives rotation.</li> <li>What's offline-first? Local data is the source of truth; sync later.</li> <li>What's a BFF? A backend per client type shaping data for its screens.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>MVP vs MVVM? Presenter calls the view vs view observes ViewModel state.</li> <li>What's unidirectional data flow? Events → logic → state → view, one direction.</li> <li>How do you handle one-off events? As effects/channels, not persistent state.</li> <li>How do you avoid stale responses? Request IDs or cancellation (<code>collectLatest</code>).</li> <li>Where does navigation logic go? A coordinator or navigation graph.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Design offline-first for a shopping list shared by family members. Local DB, outbox with idempotent ops, server merge or CRDT, conflict UI.</li> <li>MVI vs MVVM for a large app? MVI for complex async screens; MVVM with single state for simpler ones; consistency matters most.</li> <li>How do you survive process death? SavedStateHandle with IDs, re-fetch data, test with "Don't keep activities".</li> <li>When is a BFF a bad idea? Single client, or when it accumulates business rules duplicated across BFFs.</li> <li>How do you share UI logic across Android and iOS? KMP/Dart shared ViewModels, pure reducers.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add auto-refresh to <code>OrderTrackingStoreDemo</code>: a <code>Tick</code> intent every 30 seconds that refreshes only when the screen is <code>SHOWING</code> and the order isn't delivered.</p> Solution <pre><code>record Tick() implements Intent {}
// in reduce():
case Tick t -&gt; s.phase() == Phase.SHOWING &amp;&amp; !"DELIVERED".equals(s.status())
        ? load(s)
        : new Next(s, List.of());
// the timer lives outside the reducer: a scheduler dispatches new Tick() every 30 s
// while the screen is visible, and is cancelled when it isn't.
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's a fluent interface? Chained calls that read like a sentence.</li> <li>Why version an API? To make breaking changes without breaking existing clients.</li> <li>Why paginate? To return large collections in bounded chunks.</li> <li>What's idempotent? Same effect whether done once or many times.</li> <li>Is adding a response field breaking? No, for tolerant clients.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Offset vs cursor pagination? Skip N vs continue after last key; cursor is stable and fast.</li> <li>Which HTTP methods are idempotent? GET, PUT, DELETE (and HEAD, OPTIONS); POST isn't.</li> <li>How do idempotency keys work? Store response per key; replay on retry.</li> <li>What's a staged builder? A builder with an interface per step to enforce order.</li> <li>Is a new enum value breaking? Potentially, for strict clients.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you rename a field safely? Expand and contract.</li> <li>URI vs header versioning? Visibility and routing vs clean URIs and per-resource; both fine if consistent.</li> <li>Concurrent requests with the same key? First wins; others get 409 while in progress.</li> <li>How do you decide when to sunset v1? Usage metrics per client version, published policy, Sunset headers.</li> <li>What's Hyrum's Law? All observable behaviour gets depended on.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a server-side cap to <code>OrderHistoryApiDemo.list</code>: clamp <code>limit</code> to 1..50 and default to 20 when it's 0 or missing.</p> Solution <pre><code>static int effectiveLimit(Integer requested) {
    if (requested == null || requested == 0) return 20;
    return Math.max(1, Math.min(50, requested));   // never let a client ask for 100,000 rows
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's layered architecture? Layers (presentation, service, data) each calling the one below.</li> <li>Why separate layers? Separation of concerns and reuse.</li> <li>What's an adapter? Code that connects the app to external technology.</li> <li>What's a monolith? One deployable application.</li> <li>What's an event? A fact that happened, published for others to react to.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Hexagonal vs layered? Hexagonal: core defines ports, infrastructure depends on the core, not vice versa.</li> <li>What's the dependency rule? Dependencies point inwards to the domain.</li> <li>What's a modular monolith? Business modules with APIs inside one deployable.</li> <li>Event notification vs state transfer? ID only + callback vs data in the event.</li> <li>How do you enforce boundaries? ArchUnit / Spring Modulith tests in CI.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Monolith or microservices for a new product with 8 engineers? Modular monolith, extract later along proven seams.</li> <li>How do you keep events consistent with the database? After-commit publication or outbox, idempotent consumers.</li> <li>When is Clean Architecture overkill? Simple CRUD, short-lived services, tiny teams.</li> <li>How does team structure influence architecture? Conway's Law; align modules with team ownership.</li> <li>How do you evolve a big ball of mud? Identify modules, add fitness functions, strangle incrementally.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Extend the fitness check in <code>ModularMonolithFitnessDemo</code> to forbid any module from depending on more than two others.</p> Solution <pre><code>static List&lt;String&gt; fanOut(List&lt;Module&gt; modules, int max) {
    return modules.stream()
            .filter(m -&gt; m.allowedDependencies().size() &gt; max)
            .map(m -&gt; m.name() + " depends on " + m.allowedDependencies().size() + " modules (max " + max + ")")
            .toList();
}
// in verify(): problems.addAll(fanOut(modules, 2));
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Why cache? Lower latency, less load on slow stores.</li> <li>What's a TTL? Time after which an entry expires.</li> <li>What's cache-aside? The app loads on a miss and invalidates on write.</li> <li>What's a cache hit rate? Hits / (hits + misses).</li> <li>What's eviction? Removing entries when the cache is full.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Write-through vs write-behind? Synchronous store write vs asynchronous batched.</li> <li>Why delete instead of update on write? Concurrent writers can leave the older value.</li> <li>What's a cache stampede? Many misses loading the same key at once.</li> <li>Caffeine vs Redis? In-process, per JVM vs shared over network.</li> <li>What's negative caching? Caching "not found" briefly.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you keep L1 caches consistent across instances? Broadcast invalidation + short TTL.</li> <li>How do you prevent stampedes across instances? Single-flight per JVM, distributed lock or refresh-ahead, jitter.</li> <li>When is caching the wrong answer? Strong consistency needs, low hit rates, already fast queries.</li> <li>What's W-TinyLFU? Frequency-based admission with a sketch; resists scans.</li> <li>How do you roll out a change to cached formats? Versioned keys.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add refresh-ahead to <code>CategoryMenuCacheDemo</code>: after 240 seconds, return the cached menu but schedule a reload.</p> Solution <pre><code>static boolean refreshScheduled;
static List&lt;String&gt; menu(long now) {
    if (cachedMenu == null || now - cachedAt &gt;= TTL_SECONDS) return reload(now);
    if (now - cachedAt &gt;= 240 &amp;&amp; !refreshScheduled) { refreshScheduled = true; executor.execute(() -&gt; reload(now)); }
    return cachedMenu;   // callers never wait for a reload once the menu is cached
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's a ubiquitous language? A shared vocabulary used by experts and in code.</li> <li>What's a bounded context? The boundary within which a model is consistent.</li> <li>Can "product" mean different things? Yes, in different contexts.</li> <li>What does an ACL do? Translates a foreign model into ours.</li> <li>What's a published language? A documented exchange format.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Shared kernel vs shared library? Shared kernel is small, deliberately co-owned; libraries often grow into shared models.</li> <li>Conformist vs ACL? Adopt upstream's model vs translate it.</li> <li>How do you find context boundaries? Language shifts, experts, change rates, event storming.</li> <li>What's a tolerant reader? A consumer that ignores unknown fields and defaults optional ones.</li> <li>Should contexts share database tables? No.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do contexts relate to teams and services? One team per context; context may be one or more services or a module.</li> <li>How do you evolve an event schema safely? Additive changes, versions, upcasting, consumer-driven contracts, registry compatibility.</li> <li>Customer/Supplier isn't working; what now? Escalate priorities, or switch to ACL / separate ways.</li> <li>Where do you invest DDD effort? Core subdomains; buy generic ones.</li> <li>How do you migrate from a shared God model? Identify contexts, introduce context-specific models with ACLs, strangle the shared model.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a schema compatibility rule to <code>ContextIntegrationDemo</code>: a new version may not make a previously optional field required.</p> Solution <pre><code>static List&lt;String&gt; optionalBecameRequired(Schema previous, Schema proposed) {
    return previous.optional().stream()
            .filter(proposed.required()::contains)
            .sorted()
            .map(f -&gt; "'" + f + "' changed from optional to required")
            .toList();
}
// old producers (and old messages in the broker) may not include it; upcasters would need a default
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Entity vs value object? Identity vs value equality.</li> <li>Why make value objects immutable? Safe sharing, no surprise changes.</li> <li>What's an aggregate root? The entry point that enforces the aggregate's rules.</li> <li>What's a domain event? A business fact that happened, in the past tense.</li> <li>What's a repository in DDD? Collection-like access to whole aggregates.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why reference other aggregates by ID? To avoid cross-aggregate changes and huge object graphs.</li> <li>Domain service vs application service? Business rules vs orchestration/transactions.</li> <li>When are domain events dispatched? After the aggregate is saved (commit).</li> <li>How do you protect invariants? Encapsulation: private state, read-only views, methods that check rules.</li> <li>Why typed IDs? Type safety: can't pass an <code>OrderId</code> where a <code>CustomerId</code> is expected.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you choose aggregate boundaries? True invariants + contention patterns; keep them small.</li> <li>What if a business rule spans two aggregates? Eventual consistency with events and compensation, or reconsider the boundary.</li> <li>How do you handle concurrent edits? Optimistic locking with conflict handling in the UI.</li> <li>When is tactical DDD overkill? CRUD and reporting; little behaviour to model.</li> <li>How do you persist rich models with JPA without leaking? Embeddables, field access, protected constructors, or separate persistence models.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a <code>changeLitres(Litres, LocalDate effectiveFrom)</code> rule to <code>Subscription</code>: changes take effect from the day after tomorrow at the earliest, and record a <code>LitresChanged</code> event.</p> Solution <pre><code>record LitresChanged(SubscriptionId id, int litres, LocalDate from) implements Event {}   // add to the sealed list

void changeLitres(Litres newLitres, LocalDate from, LocalDate today) {
    if (from.isBefore(today.plusDays(2))) throw new IllegalStateException("changes apply from " + today.plusDays(2));
    if (newLitres.equals(litres)) return;
    litres = newLitres;            // a fuller model would keep a schedule of changes by date
    events.add(new LitresChanged(id, newLitres.value(), from));
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Why not one transaction across services? Each owns its database; 2PC couples availability.</li> <li>What's a compensating action? A business undo, like a refund.</li> <li>What's the outbox? A table written in the same transaction as the data, published later.</li> <li>What's CQRS? Separate models for writes and reads.</li> <li>What does idempotent mean? Doing it twice has the same effect as once.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Orchestration vs choreography? Central coordinator vs services reacting to events.</li> <li>Why is outbox delivery at least once? The relay can crash after publishing, before marking.</li> <li>How does a projection handle out-of-order events? Versions: skip old, park future.</li> <li>Event sourcing concurrency? Expected-version appends.</li> <li>What is CDC? Streaming committed changes from the database log.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you handle saga isolation anomalies? Semantic locks, pending states, commutative updates, re-reads.</li> <li>Where do you place irreversible steps? After the pivot, at the end, and make them retriable.</li> <li>Event sourcing and GDPR? PII outside events or crypto-shredding.</li> <li>Exactly-once processing? At-least-once delivery + idempotent effects; Kafka EOS only inside Kafka.</li> <li>Raw CDC vs outbox events? Outbox is a designed contract; raw CDC leaks schema.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>TransactionalOutboxDemo</code> deliver each event at most once to a consumer even though the relay can publish twice.</p> Solution <pre><code>Set&lt;String&gt; processed = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();
for (String event : broker) {
    if (!processed.add(event)) continue;   // the event ID (here the text) is the dedupe key
    System.out.println("consumer handled " + event);
}
// in production: an event ID column in the outbox, and a processed-messages table on the consumer side
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Queue vs topic? One receiver vs every subscriber.</li> <li>Why use messaging? Decoupling in time, speed and knowledge.</li> <li>What's a dead letter queue? Where unprocessable messages go.</li> <li>What's a correlation ID? An ID linking a reply to its request.</li> <li>What's a message translator? Converts one format to another.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you keep ordering with competing consumers? Partition by key.</li> <li>Content-based router vs recipient list? One destination vs several.</li> <li>When to commit offsets? After processing.</li> <li>What's a poison message? One that fails every time; dead-letter it.</li> <li>Kafka consumer group? Consumers sharing partitions; each group gets every message.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Retry topics vs blocking retries? Retry topics keep the partition moving but break per-key order; blocking keeps order but stalls.</li> <li>Delivery guarantees? At-most, at-least, effectively-once via idempotency.</li> <li>When would you choose RabbitMQ over Kafka? Routing-heavy work queues, per-message acks, low volume; Kafka for event logs, replay, high throughput.</li> <li>Large payloads? Claim check.</li> <li>Canonical data model risks? Becomes a company-wide God model; keep it per context.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a message filter to <code>ContentBasedRouterDemo</code> that drops test orders (IDs starting with <code>TEST-</code>) before routing, and counts them.</p> Solution <pre><code>int dropped = 0;
void accept(Order o) {
    if (o.id().startsWith("TEST-")) { dropped++; return; }   // filter: never reaches any channel
    route(o);
}
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's a microservice? An independently deployable service owning one capability and its data.</li> <li>What does an API gateway do? Single entry point: routing, auth, rate limits.</li> <li>Why database per service? So services change and scale independently.</li> <li>What's service discovery? Finding live instances of a service dynamically.</li> <li>What's the strangler fig? Incrementally replacing a legacy system behind a facade.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Entity vs capability decomposition? Entities cause chatty call chains; capabilities keep use cases local.</li> <li>How does a service get another service's data? API call, or a local copy via events.</li> <li>Client-side vs server-side discovery? Caller load-balances vs platform/LB does.</li> <li>What's a sidecar? A helper container alongside the app for cross-cutting concerns.</li> <li>What's a BFF? A gateway per client type shaped for that client.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>When would you not use microservices? Small team, unclear domain, immature ops; use a modular monolith.</li> <li>How do you prevent a distributed monolith? Capability boundaries, own data, async integration, contracts.</li> <li>How do you migrate a monolith safely? Strangler fig, shadow traffic, incremental cut-over, delete legacy.</li> <li>How do you design a composed endpoint for resilience? Parallel calls, one deadline, required vs optional, degraded responses.</li> <li>App retries vs mesh retries? One owner per concern; avoid multiplying retries.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Change <code>SlotsExtractionDemo</code> to cut over gradually: route a percentage of PIN codes to the new service based on a stable hash.</p> Solution <pre><code>int rolloutPercent = 10;
String slotFor(String pin) {
    boolean inRollout = Math.floorMod(pin.hashCode(), 100) &lt; rolloutPercent;
    return inRollout ? candidate.apply(pin) : MONOLITH.apply(pin);
}
// a stable hash keeps each customer on one side, so they don't see answers flip between requests
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Why set timeouts? So a slow dependency can't hang the caller.</li> <li>What's exponential backoff? Waiting longer after each failed attempt.</li> <li>What's a circuit breaker? Stops calling a failing service for a while.</li> <li>What's a fallback? A substitute answer when the call fails.</li> <li>What does a rate limiter do? Caps calls per time unit.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why jitter? To avoid synchronised retries (thundering herd).</li> <li>Which errors should be retried? Transient ones (timeouts, 503, resets) on idempotent operations.</li> <li>Circuit breaker states? Closed, open, half-open.</li> <li>Semaphore vs thread-pool bulkhead? Limit callers vs separate executor.</li> <li>Token bucket vs fixed window? Bursts with steady refill vs simple counters with edge bursts.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Order of Resilience4j decorators? Retry(CircuitBreaker(RateLimiter(TimeLimiter(Bulkhead(call))))), so each attempt is counted and limited.</li> <li>How do you prevent retry storms? One layer, jitter, budget, breaker, Retry-After.</li> <li>How do you make payment retries safe? Idempotency keys deduplicated by the provider or your service.</li> <li>Bulkheads with virtual threads? Still needed: semaphores protect dependencies when threads are unlimited.</li> <li>What's a metastable failure? A system stuck in overload after the trigger is gone, sustained by retries.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a slow-call rule to <code>CircuitBreakerDemo</code>: calls slower than 1 s count as failures.</p> Solution <pre><code>record Result(String body, long latencyMs) {}
// in call(): measure latency (or take it from the reply) and record a failure if it's too slow
boolean ok = threw == null &amp;&amp; result.latencyMs() &lt;= 1_000;
// Resilience4j equivalent: slowCallDurationThreshold(Duration.ofSeconds(1)) and slowCallRateThreshold(50)
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's a fitness function? An automated check of an architectural characteristic.</li> <li>What's ArchUnit? A library for architecture rules as unit tests.</li> <li>What does <code>exports</code> do in module-info? Makes a package visible to other modules.</li> <li>What's coupling? How much modules depend on each other.</li> <li>Why avoid cycles between modules? They can't be changed or released independently.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Atomic vs holistic fitness functions? One characteristic vs a combination.</li> <li>How do you adopt rules on legacy code? Freeze existing violations; ratchet down.</li> <li>What's instability? Ce / (Ca + Ce).</li> <li><code>requires transitive</code>? Re-exports a dependency to your consumers.</li> <li>ArchUnit vs JPMS? Test-time rules vs compiler/runtime enforcement.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Zone of pain? Stable and concrete: many dependants, hard to change.</li> <li>How do you keep performance fitness functions reliable? Repeated runs, robust stats, inconclusive outcomes, dedicated runners.</li> <li>Why not JPMS for every Spring Boot app? Reflection-heavy frameworks, migration cost; ArchUnit/Modulith give most value.</li> <li>What's change coupling? Files that change together in history.</li> <li>How do fitness functions relate to ADRs? ADR records the decision; fitness function enforces it.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Add a rule to <code>ArchitectureFitnessSuiteDemo</code> that fails when any module has more than 3 outgoing dependencies.</p> Solution <pre><code>graph.forEach((module, deps) -&gt; {
    if (deps.size() &gt; 3) violations.add(new Violation("max-dependencies", module + " depends on " + deps.size() + " modules"));
});
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's an SPI? An interface for others to implement, discovered at run time.</li> <li>What does ServiceLoader read? <code>META-INF/services/&lt;interface&gt;</code> files (or module <code>provides</code>).</li> <li>What is inversion of control? The framework calls your code.</li> <li>Why use static factories? Hide the implementation class, return interfaces.</li> <li>What does semantic versioning encode? MAJOR breaking, MINOR additive, PATCH fixes.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>API vs SPI? Callers use the API; extenders implement the SPI.</li> <li>Is adding a default method safe? Yes, binary compatible (rare source conflicts).</li> <li>What does <code>exports ... to</code> do? Exports a package only to named modules.</li> <li>Why do plugins declare an API version? So the host can reject incompatible plugins at load time.</li> <li>Why is classpath order a bad priority mechanism? It's not guaranteed; add explicit priorities.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Source vs binary compatibility? Whether callers recompile fine vs whether compiled callers still link.</li> <li>Why does widening int to long break? The method descriptor changes; old binaries look for the old one.</li> <li>Why are changed constants dangerous? Inlined into callers at compile time.</li> <li>How do you evolve an SPI interface? Default methods, abstract base classes, or a new versioned interface.</li> <li>What's Hyrum's law? All observable behaviour gets depended on.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>ServiceLoaderDemo</code> choose the cheapest partner, with a deterministic tie-break, instead of relying on file order.</p> Solution <pre><code>DeliveryPartner best = null;
for (var it = partners.iterator(); ; ) {
    try {
        if (!it.hasNext()) break;
        DeliveryPartner p = it.next();
        if (best == null
                || p.quotePaise(5) &lt; best.quotePaise(5)
                || (p.quotePaise(5) == best.quotePaise(5) &amp;&amp; p.name().compareTo(best.name()) &lt; 0)) best = p;
    } catch (ServiceConfigurationError e) { /* log and skip */ }
}
System.out.println("cheapest: " + best.name());
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>What's a pattern catalogue? A list of approved and avoided patterns with guidance.</li> <li>What's a reference implementation? A working example of the approved way.</li> <li>What's technical debt? Shortcuts whose cost grows over time.</li> <li>Why use a PR checklist? To make required steps explicit and checkable.</li> <li>What's a guild? A cross-team community around a skill.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>ADOPT vs TRIAL vs HOLD? Default vs allowed with review vs no new uses.</li> <li>Why risk-based review? Fast path for safe changes, attention for risky ones.</li> <li>Principal vs interest? Cost to fix vs ongoing cost of not fixing.</li> <li>How do you roll out a new lint rule on old code? Ratchet: fail only new violations.</li> <li>Why do templates drift? Copied once; fixes don't propagate.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you avoid governance becoming a bottleneck? Automate, route by risk, federate ownership.</li> <li>How do you prioritise debt? Payback period, hotspots, fixed capacity share.</li> <li>What's Goodhart's law here? Targeted metrics get gamed; combine and guard them.</li> <li>Paved road vs gate? Make the right way easiest instead of blocking alternatives.</li> <li>How do you retire a standard? Owner review, ADR superseding it, update lint and references.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Extend <code>TechDebtStrategyDemo</code> so items in hotspot files (changed more than 20 times last quarter) get their interest doubled before ranking.</p> Solution <pre><code>record Debt(String item, int principalDays, double interestDaysPerMonth, int changesLastQuarter) {
    double effectiveInterest() { return changesLastQuarter &gt; 20 ? interestDaysPerMonth * 2 : interestDaysPerMonth; }
    double paybackMonths() { return principalDays / effectiveInterest(); }
}
// the God class with 35 changes now ranks above the retry loops
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>When is a class initialised? On first active use, once per class loader.</li> <li>What's a dynamic proxy? A runtime-generated class routing calls to a handler.</li> <li>Is a lambda an inner class? No; it's linked via invokedynamic to a hidden class.</li> <li>What does volatile guarantee? Visibility and ordering of reads and writes to that field.</li> <li>Why are immutable objects thread-safe? State can't change after safe construction.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Why is the holder idiom thread-safe? Class initialisation is locked and happens once.</li> <li>JDK proxy vs CGLIB? Interfaces vs subclass; final methods can't be proxied by subclassing.</li> <li>Why doesn't @Transactional work on self-invocation? The call doesn't go through the proxy.</li> <li>MethodHandle vs reflection? Typed, checked once, inlinable vs checked per call.</li> <li>Why does DCL need volatile? Without it, another thread can see a partly constructed object.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How can there be two singletons? Two class loaders.</li> <li>What does JLS 17.5 guarantee? Final fields visible after construction, if <code>this</code> didn't escape.</li> <li>What's a hidden class? A non-discoverable class defined at runtime, e.g. for lambdas.</li> <li>What do VarHandles add over volatile? Per-access memory modes and CAS on fields and arrays.</li> <li>How do you diagnose a class loader leak? Metaspace growth, heap dump GC roots of the loader, jcmd VM.classloaders.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Make <code>AuditProxyDemo</code> audit the inner <code>setPrice</code> calls too, without changing the proxy.</p> Solution <pre><code>// Give the service a reference to its own proxy and call through it:
static final class PriceAdminService implements PriceAdmin {
    PriceAdmin self = this;                 // replaced with the proxy after creation
    public void bulkDiscount(List&lt;String&gt; skus, int percent) {
        for (String s : skus) self.setPrice(s, prices.get(s) * (100 - percent) / 100);
    }
}
service.self = admin;   // now inner calls are audited (Spring: inject the bean into itself or split beans)
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Why avoid big-bang rewrites? High risk, late value, missed rules.</li> <li>What's the strangler fig? Replace a system piece by piece behind a router.</li> <li>What's a feature toggle? A runtime switch for behaviour.</li> <li>What's a seam? A place to change behaviour without editing the code there.</li> <li>What's a parallel run? Old and new on the same inputs, compared.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>Branch by abstraction steps? Abstraction, move callers, new impl, switch, delete.</li> <li>Why serve the legacy result in a parallel run? It's trusted; the new one isn't yet.</li> <li>What does a legacy ACL handle? Formats, codes, implied decimals, dates, validation.</li> <li>Toggle types? Release, ops, experiment, permission.</li> <li>Why stable hashing for rollouts? Each customer stays in one variant.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you migrate data with no downtime? Source of truth, idempotent copy, backfill, reconcile, switch reads, switch writes.</li> <li>When is a strangler slice done? New side owns data and the legacy path is deleted.</li> <li>How do you decide promotion? Sample size, zero unexplained diffs, segment coverage, rollback criteria.</li> <li>How do you prevent toggle debt? Types, owners, expiry, CI checks.</li> <li>What's transitional architecture? Temporary routing and sync code with planned removal.</li> </ol> <p>Task: In <code>ParallelRunDemo</code>, normalise before comparing: treat any difference of at most 100 paise on subscriber orders as accepted, and report the maximum accepted difference.</p> Solution <pre><code>long maxAccepted = 0;
long diff = Math.abs(served - candidate);
if (o.subscriber() &amp;&amp; diff &lt;= 100) { category = "accepted"; maxAccepted = Math.max(maxAccepted, diff); }
System.out.println("largest accepted difference: " + maxAccepted + " paise");
</code></pre>

🟢 L1–L2🟡 L3🔴 L4–L5💻 Hands-on <ol> <li>Is using more patterns better? No; each adds cost.</li> <li>What's YAGNI? "You aren't gonna need it": don't build for speculative needs.</li> <li>What's an ADR? A short record of a decision and its reasons.</li> <li>What's the rule of three? Abstract at the third occurrence.</li> <li>What's a trade-off? Gaining one quality at the cost of another.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>How do you decide whether to introduce Strategy? Variants, change frequency, ownership, testing needs.</li> <li>What goes in an ADR? Context, decision, consequences, status.</li> <li>How do you change an accepted ADR? Supersede it with a new one.</li> <li>What's a decision matrix's main risk? False precision; tuned weights.</li> <li>Runtime cost of indirection? Usually negligible; cognitive cost dominates.</li> </ol> <ol> <li>One-way vs two-way doors? Irreversible vs cheap to reverse; match process to reversibility.</li> <li>How do you make a one-way door two-way? Seams, flags, expand-and-contract, abstractions at volatile boundaries.</li> <li>How do you keep ADRs useful at scale? In repo, cited from code, validated in CI, review dates.</li> <li>How do you handle a close decision? Find the criterion it hinges on; discuss that; prototype.</li> <li>When do you remove a pattern? When its variation never materialised and it slows change.</li> </ol> <p>Task: Extend <code>AdrLogDemo</code> to flag two Accepted ADRs whose titles both start with "Delivery fees" (likely conflicting decisions).</p> Solution <pre><code>Map&lt;String, List&lt;Integer&gt;&gt; byTopic = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();
for (Adr a : index.values())
    if (a.status().equals("Accepted"))
        byTopic.computeIfAbsent(a.title().split(" as ")[0], k -&gt; new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()).add(a.number());
byTopic.forEach((topic, ids) -&gt; { if (ids.size() &gt; 1) problems.add("possible conflict on '" + topic + "': " + ids); });
</code></pre>

FreshCartCapstoneTest.java<pre><code>import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertInstanceOf;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertSame;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Set;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class FreshCartCapstoneTest {

    static final FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Product MILK = new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Product("MILK-1L", "Milk", new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Money(6800), Set.of("dairy"));
    static final FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Product SPINACH = new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Product("SPINACH-250G", "Spinach", new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Money(6000), Set.of("organic"));

    FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Stock stock;
    FreshCartCapstoneDemo.PayLink payLink;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        stock = new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Stock();
        stock.units.putAll(Map.of("MILK-1L", 10, "SPINACH-250G", 10));
        payLink = new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.PayLink();
    }

    private FreshCartCapstoneDemo.CheckoutFacade facade(FreshCartCapstoneDemo.DeliveryPartner quickDrop) {
        return new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.CheckoutFacade(stock, payLink,
                new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.PartnerRouter(quickDrop, new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.ParcelGoAdapter(new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.ParcelGoApi())));
    }

    private static FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Order order(String id, int milk, int spinach) {
        return new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Order(id, "asha", "Bengaluru",
                List.of(new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Line(MILK, milk), new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Line(SPINACH, spinach)));
    }

    @Test
    void lineQuantityInvariantIsEnforced() {
        assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -&gt; new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Line(MILK, 21));
    }

    @Test
    void couponDiscountsOnlyOrganicItemsWhenEligible() {
        var quote = FreshCartCapstoneDemo.price(order("A", 2, 3), FreshCartCapstoneDemo.GREEN10, FreshCartCapstoneDemo.STANDARD_FEE);
        assertEquals(1800, quote.discount().paise());                // 10% of 3 x Rs 60
        var small = FreshCartCapstoneDemo.price(order("B", 1, 1), FreshCartCapstoneDemo.GREEN10, FreshCartCapstoneDemo.STANDARD_FEE);
        assertEquals(0, small.discount().paise());                   // basket under Rs 300
    }

    @Test
    void sameIdempotencyKeyChargesOnce() {
        var checkout = facade(new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.QuickDropAdapter(new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.QuickDropApi()));
        var order = order("A", 1, 1);
        var first = checkout.checkout("k1", order, null);
        assertSame(first, checkout.checkout("k1", order, null));
        assertEquals(1, payLink.ledger.size());
    }

    @Test
    void failedDeliveryBookingRefundsAndReleasesStock() {
        var broken = new FreshCartCapstoneDemo.DeliveryPartner() {
            public String name() { return "QuickDrop"; }
            public String book(String orderId) { throw new IllegalStateException("QuickDrop unavailable"); }
        };
        var receipt = facade(broken).checkout("k2", order("A", 2, 1), null);
        assertEquals("CANCELLED", receipt.status());
        assertEquals(List.of("charge A Rs 225.00", "refund PL-A Rs 225.00"), payLink.ledger);
        assertEquals(10, stock.units.get("MILK-1L"));
    }

    @Test
    void cancelledOrderCannotBeDispatched() {
        var order = order("A", 1, 1);
        order.cancel("customer request");
        assertInstanceOf(FreshCartCapstoneDemo.Cancelled.class, order.state());
        assertThrows(IllegalStateException.class, () -&gt; order.markDispatched("QuickDrop"));
    }
}
